/* TIH — Authored per-topic teaching notes for the Digital SAT course.
   The course player renders these directly instead of the generic lesson
   template. Keyed by topic title; numbering, leading emoji and case are
   ignored when matching. A key may be module-qualified ("M9:Radicals")
   where the same title is taught twice with a different emphasis. */
(function () {
  if (typeof window === 'undefined') return;
  window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES = window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES || {};
  window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES['sat'] = {
  "Welcome to the Course": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Welcome to the Course</strong><br><span>A clear path from where you are now to the score your college asks for.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Welcome to the Course\"><figcaption>Welcome to the Course: know the test and prepare</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Starting point</strong><span>Take a diagnostic and record your baseline.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Target</strong><span>Set a score goal linked to admission needs.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Routine</strong><span>Learn → practise → review → repeat.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Digital SAT</summary><p>The computer-delivered SAT used to measure college-readiness skills.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>baseline score</summary><p>A starting score used to identify present strengths and gaps.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>score goal</summary><p>A specific result a learner aims to achieve.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>diagnostic test</summary><p>An assessment used to identify areas requiring study.</p></details></section><p>This course prepares you for the <strong>Digital SAT</strong>, the version College Board has used worldwide since 2023. Everything here matches that test: two sections, adaptive modules, the Bluebook app, and a 400&ndash;1600 total.</p><p>Work in order. Modules 1&ndash;6 build Reading &amp; Writing, 7&ndash;11 build Math, 12 covers digital test strategy, and 13 is where you sit full mock exams. Each lesson is a video, a note like this one, and a short quiz you must pass to unlock the next lesson.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>The Digital SAT is <strong>2 hours 14 minutes</strong> of testing, not the old 3 hours.</li><li>There is <strong>no penalty for a wrong answer</strong>, so you answer every question.</li><li>Colleges see your total (400&ndash;1600) and your two section scores (200&ndash;800 each).</li><li>Consistency beats intensity: one lesson a day for eight weeks beats one long weekend.</li><li>Keep an error log from lesson 1. It becomes the most valuable file you own.</li><li>You need the Final Assessment in Module 13 to earn your certificate.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Fatu in Monrovia wants a 1200. She takes a diagnostic in week one, scores 980, and sees that Math costs her more than Reading. She spends four evenings a week on Math and two on Reading &amp; Writing, and re-tests every fortnight rather than guessing whether she has improved.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Most candidates who miss their target did not lack ability. They studied whatever felt comfortable instead of whatever was costing them points, and they found out too late.</p></div>",
  "What is the SAT?": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 What is the SAT?</strong><br><span>One standardised test that lets colleges compare applicants from anywhere.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for What is the SAT?\"><figcaption>What is the SAT?: know the test and prepare</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Purpose</strong><span>Measures college-ready reading, writing, and mathematics.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Sections</strong><span>Reading & Writing + Math.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Use</strong><span>One part of a wider college application.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>SAT</summary><p>A standardized college-admission assessment administered by College Board.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>standardized test</summary><p>An assessment given and scored under consistent conditions.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>college readiness</summary><p>The knowledge and skills needed for entry-level college work.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>admission</summary><p>The process through which an institution accepts applicants.</p></details></section><p>The SAT is a college admission test run by the College Board. It measures reading, writing and mathematical reasoning &mdash; not what any one school happened to teach you &mdash; so an applicant from Liberia and one from Ohio can be compared on the same scale.</p><p>Most United States colleges accept it, and many scholarship programmes use it as a cut-off. Some colleges are now <em>test-optional</em>, but a strong score still helps, and for international applicants seeking funding it often matters more, not less.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>The SAT tests <strong>reasoning</strong> with reading, writing and maths, not memorised facts.</li><li>It is written and administered by the <strong>College Board</strong>.</li><li>Scores are used for admission, scholarships and sometimes course placement.</li><li>SAT scores are generally valid for <strong>five years</strong>.</li><li>Test-optional means a college will consider you without a score &mdash; not that a good score is ignored.</li><li>The SAT is not the ACT; they are separate tests and most colleges accept either.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A student applying to five American universities finds three are test-optional and two require a score. She sits the SAT anyway: the two requiring it will not read her file without one, and one of the test-optional three awards merit scholarships using SAT bands.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Knowing what the test is for tells you how hard to push. A score that only unlocks admission is a different target from one that has to win you funding.</p></div>",
  "SAT Test Format": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 SAT Test Format</strong><br><span>Two sections, four modules, and a structure that never changes.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for SAT Test Format\"><figcaption>SAT Test Format: know the test and prepare</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reading & Writing</strong><span>64 minutes · 54 questions.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Math</strong><span>70 minutes · 44 questions.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Total</strong><span>2 hours 14 minutes plus a break.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>module</summary><p>One timed part of a Digital SAT section.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>section</summary><p>A major tested area: Reading and Writing or Math.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>multiple choice</summary><p>A question answered by selecting from listed options.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>student-produced response</summary><p>A Math answer entered by the test taker rather than selected.</p></details></section><p>The Digital SAT has two sections: <strong>Reading &amp; Writing</strong> and <strong>Math</strong>. Each section is split into two modules, and each module is separately timed. Reading &amp; Writing gives you 27 questions in 32 minutes per module; Math gives you 22 questions in 35 minutes per module.</p><p>Reading &amp; Writing questions are short: one passage of about 25&ndash;150 words with a single question attached. You never face a long passage with ten questions hanging off it, the way the paper test worked.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Reading &amp; Writing</strong> &ndash; 2 modules, 27 questions each, 32 minutes each.</li><li><strong>Math</strong> &ndash; 2 modules, 22 questions each, 35 minutes each.</li><li>There is a <strong>10-minute break</strong> between the two sections.</li><li>Each Reading &amp; Writing question has its own short passage.</li><li>About 75% of Math is multiple choice; the rest are student-produced responses you type in.</li><li>A built-in Desmos graphing calculator is available for the whole Math section.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">In Reading &amp; Writing you might see a 60-word paragraph about cassava farming followed by one question on the author's main point &mdash; then a completely unrelated 40-word paragraph for the next question. Short, self-contained, and fast.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Knowing the format removes surprise on test day, and surprise is expensive: a candidate expecting long passages wastes the first minutes of module one adjusting instead of answering.</p></div>",
  "SAT Scoring System (400–1600)": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 SAT Scoring System (400–1600)</strong><br><span>Two section scores, added together, and nothing subtracted for a wrong answer.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for SAT Scoring System (400–1600)\"><figcaption>SAT Scoring System (400–1600): know the test and prepare</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reading & Writing</strong><span>200–800.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Math</strong><span>200–800.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Total score</strong><span>400–1600.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>scaled score</summary><p>A score converted from performance using an established scoring process.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>section score</summary><p>The 200–800 result for one SAT section.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>total score</summary><p>The sum of the two section scores.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>score range</summary><p>The lowest through highest possible score values.</p></details></section><p>Reading &amp; Writing is scored 200&ndash;800 and Math is scored 200&ndash;800. Your total is simply the two added, so the scale runs 400&ndash;1600. There is no separate essay score; the essay was retired.</p><p>Raw correct answers are converted to scaled scores through a process called <strong>equating</strong>, which adjusts for small differences in difficulty between test forms. This is why you cannot compute your score by counting correct answers, and why a slightly harder form does not disadvantage you.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Total = Reading &amp; Writing (200&ndash;800) + Math (200&ndash;800), so 400&ndash;1600.</li><li><strong>No penalty for wrong answers</strong> &mdash; always answer, always guess if you must.</li><li>Roughly 1050 is around the national average; 1200+ is competitive; 1400+ is selective.</li><li>Equating means the same performance earns the same score on any test form.</li><li>Because the test is adaptive, harder second modules make the top of the range reachable.</li><li>You can send scores to colleges and, with Score Choice, choose which sitting to send.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Moses scores 610 Reading &amp; Writing and 590 Math. His total is 1200, which clears the 1150 cut-off for his university's international scholarship. Had he left six questions blank rather than guessing, that total would very likely have started with an 11.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Understanding the scale sets a realistic target and shows which section to work on. Adding 60 points to a weak Math score is usually far easier than squeezing the last 20 out of a strong Reading score.</p></div>",
  "Digital SAT Overview": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Digital SAT Overview</strong><br><span>The test adapts to you, and that changes how the first module matters.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Digital SAT Overview\"><figcaption>Digital SAT Overview: know the test and prepare</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Device</strong><span>Bluebook runs the exam on an approved device.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Adaptive</strong><span>Module 2 difficulty responds to Module 1 performance.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Tools</strong><span>Timer, annotation, reference sheet, and Desmos calculator.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>adaptive testing</summary><p>A format in which later question difficulty depends partly on earlier performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Bluebook</summary><p>College Board's application for taking the Digital SAT.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Desmos</summary><p>The built-in graphing calculator available during Math.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>annotation</summary><p>A digital mark or note used to identify important information.</p></details></section><p>The Digital SAT is <strong>multistage adaptive</strong>. You sit module 1 of a section; the app scores it instantly and routes you into either an easier or a harder module 2. Both routes are scored fairly, but only the harder route reaches the top of the 200&ndash;800 range.</p><p>This is not adaptive question by question &mdash; within a module you can move freely, flag questions and come back. The adaptation happens once, between the two modules.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Adaptation happens <strong>between modules</strong>, not between questions.</li><li>Module 1 performance decides whether module 2 is the easier or harder form.</li><li>Within a module you may skip, flag and return as much as you like.</li><li>The harder module 2 unlocks the upper part of the section's score range.</li><li>Both sections adapt independently: a strong Math module 1 does not affect Reading.</li><li>Never rush module 1 to save time for module 2 &mdash; module 1 sets your ceiling.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Two students each answer 30 of 54 Reading &amp; Writing questions correctly. The one who got more of them right in module 1 was routed to the harder module 2 and scores higher, because that route is scaled to a higher band. Same total correct, different score.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Most SAT advice written before 2023 assumes a fixed test where every question counts the same. On the Digital SAT, treating module 1 as the more important half is a genuine strategy change.</p></div>",
  "Study Plan": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Study Plan</strong><br><span>A plan turns a 1600-point scale into this evening's task.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Study Plan\"><figcaption>Study Plan: know the test and prepare</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Diagnose</strong><span>Rank topics by weakness and importance.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Schedule</strong><span>Use short, regular study blocks.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Measure</strong><span>Track accuracy, timing, and repeated errors.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>study plan</summary><p>A scheduled program of learning activities connected to a goal.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>spaced practice</summary><p>Study distributed across multiple sessions rather than crowded into one.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>accuracy rate</summary><p>The percentage of attempted questions answered correctly.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>error log</summary><p>A record of mistakes, causes, corrections, and review dates.</p></details></section><p>Start with a diagnostic full test so you know your real starting score and, more importantly, which section is costing you more. Then set a target and a test date and work backwards.</p><p>A workable eight-week shape: weeks 1&ndash;2 fundamentals in your weaker section, weeks 3&ndash;5 topic drills across both, weeks 6&ndash;7 timed modules, week 8 full mocks and taper. Adjust the weeks, keep the order.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Diagnose first. Studying before you know your weak section wastes the first fortnight.</li><li>Give the weaker section roughly twice the time of the stronger one.</li><li>Study in focused blocks of 45 minutes rather than open-ended sessions.</li><li>Take a full timed mock every two weeks, not every day.</li><li>Log every wrong answer with <em>why</em> it was wrong, not just the correct letter.</li><li>Do the final full mock about five days before the test, then taper.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Fatu scores 980 on her diagnostic: 540 Reading &amp; Writing, 440 Math. She gives Math four evenings a week and Reading two, keeps Sunday for reviewing her error log, and re-tests on alternate Saturdays. By week six her Math is 520 and her plan shifts to timed modules.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Without a plan, students practise what feels comfortable. The error log and the fortnightly mock are what stop that, because both make avoidance visible.</p></div>",
  "Registration Process": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Registration Process</strong><br><span>Register early, because seats at a centre run out before the deadline does.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Registration Process\"><figcaption>Registration Process: know the test and prepare</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Account</strong><span>Create or verify a College Board account.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Book</strong><span>Choose an available date and test center.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Confirm</strong><span>Check personal details, deadline, payment, and admission ticket.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>registration</summary><p>The formal process of booking a test administration.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>deadline</summary><p>The final date by which an action must be completed.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>test center</summary><p>An approved location where the SAT is administered.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>confirmation</summary><p>Official evidence that registration has been completed.</p></details></section><p>You register through your College Board account at collegeboard.org: create the account, choose a test date, choose a test centre, upload a photo and pay the fee. International test centres are fewer and fill quickly, so book as soon as dates open.</p><p>The photo you upload has to match the identity document you will bring. Your name on the registration must match that document exactly &mdash; a middle name on one and not the other is enough to cause trouble at the door.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Register through your own <strong>College Board account</strong>, not a third party.</li><li>International seats are limited; book as early as dates are released.</li><li>Your registration name must match your <strong>passport</strong> exactly.</li><li>The uploaded photo must be a clear, recent head-and-shoulders image of you.</li><li>Note the regular deadline and the late-registration deadline &mdash; late costs extra.</li><li>Print or save the admission ticket; you need it with your ID on the day.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A student registers as <em>Musu Kollie</em> but her passport reads <em>Musu B. Kollie</em>. She corrects the registration a week before the date. Had she left it, the centre could have refused her entry with no refund.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Registration is the cheapest part of the process to get right and among the most expensive to get wrong: a rejected entry means paying again and waiting months for the next international date.</p></div>",
  "Test-Day Rules": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Test-Day Rules</strong><br><span>The rules are simple, and breaking one can void a score you already earned.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Test-Day Rules\"><figcaption>Test-Day Rules: know the test and prepare</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Bring</strong><span>Approved ID, admission information, device, and charger.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Follow</strong><span>Arrival, seating, break, and calculator instructions.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Avoid</strong><span>Phones, unauthorized materials, and prohibited assistance.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>valid identification</summary><p>An accepted document confirming a test taker's identity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>prohibited device</summary><p>Technology not permitted for use during testing.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>test security</summary><p>Rules protecting the fairness and integrity of the assessment.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dismissal</summary><p>Removal from the test because of a serious rule violation.</p></details></section><p>Bring an acceptable photo ID and your admission ticket. Bring your own fully charged laptop or tablet with Bluebook installed, or use a centre device if you requested one. Bring a charger, and an approved calculator if you prefer your own to the built-in Desmos.</p><p>Phones, smart watches and any recording device must be off and away for the whole session, including the break. Being seen with one is grounds for cancelling your score, not just a warning.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Acceptable ID: a valid <strong>passport</strong> for international candidates.</li><li>Your device must be charged and have Bluebook installed and updated beforehand.</li><li>Complete <strong>exam setup</strong> in Bluebook in the days before, not on the morning.</li><li>Phones and smart watches off and stored &mdash; through the break as well.</li><li>Arrive early; doors close and latecomers are not admitted.</li><li>You may not discuss or share test content afterwards; doing so can void your score.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A candidate finishes module 1, checks the time on a smart watch during the break, and is reported. The score for a test he had already half-completed is cancelled. Nothing about his English or his maths was in question.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Every rule here is about protecting the score you spent months building. They cost nothing to follow and everything to ignore.</p></div>",
  "Using the Bluebook Testing App": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Using the Bluebook Testing App</strong><br><span>The app is not just a screen; its tools are worth points.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Using the Bluebook Testing App\"><figcaption>Using the Bluebook Testing App: know the test and prepare</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Before</strong><span>Install, sign in, update, and complete exam setup.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Practise</strong><span>Use preview tests and learn every tool.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Test day</strong><span>Open the assigned exam and follow proctor instructions.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>exam setup</summary><p>The required Bluebook process that prepares a registered test.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>device check</summary><p>Verification that a computer meets testing requirements.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>reference sheet</summary><p>A built-in list of approved Math formulas.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>proctor</summary><p>The person responsible for supervising a test session.</p></details></section><p>Bluebook is College Board's testing application, and everything happens inside it: the timer, the questions, the annotation tool, the flag tool, the reference sheet and the Desmos graphing calculator. Install it and run <em>exam setup</em> well before test day.</p><p>Learn the tools in practice, not on the day. Every minute spent working out how to graph an equation in Desmos during the test is a minute not spent answering.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Flag</strong> a hard question, move on, and return before the module ends.</li><li><strong>Annotate</strong> lets you highlight passage text and attach a note.</li><li><strong>Option eliminator</strong> crosses out answers you have ruled out.</li><li>The <strong>Desmos graphing calculator</strong> is available for the entire Math section.</li><li>A <strong>reference sheet</strong> of geometry formulas is built in &mdash; you need not memorise those.</li><li>Bluebook has full-length practice tests; use them so the interface feels ordinary.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">On a hard quadratic, instead of factoring under pressure, a student types the equation into Desmos and reads the x-intercepts off the graph in about fifteen seconds. Same answer, a fraction of the risk.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The students who gain most from the digital format are the ones who practised inside Bluebook. The tools are free, they are the same on test day, and they turn some hard questions into fast ones.</p></div>",
  "Academic Vocabulary": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Academic Vocabulary</strong><br><span>The SAT tests words you meet in real texts, not a memorised rare-word list.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-foundation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT foundation guide for Academic Vocabulary\"><figcaption>Academic Vocabulary: understand the rule and apply it</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Everyday</strong><span>The result went up.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Academic</strong><span>The result increased significantly.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Context</strong><span>The sentence determines the most precise meaning.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>academic vocabulary</summary><p>Formal words commonly used in educational and scholarly texts.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>precision</summary><p>The quality of expressing an idea exactly.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>connotation</summary><p>A feeling or association carried by a word.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>context</summary><p>The surrounding information that helps determine meaning.</p></details></section><p>The Digital SAT dropped obscure vocabulary. What it tests now is <strong>high-utility academic words</strong> &mdash; the ones that appear across history, science and social science writing: <em>undermine, plausible, arbitrary, nuanced, comprehensive, novel, sustain, warrant</em>.</p><p>These words are tested in context. You are shown a short passage with a blank or an underlined word and asked which choice best fits the meaning <em>as the author uses it</em>, which is often not the word's most common meaning.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Learn <strong>academic</strong> words, not rare ones &mdash; the test moved away from obscurity.</li><li>Many tested words have a common meaning and a scholarly one; the scholarly one is usually correct.</li><li>Learn each word with a sentence, never as a bare definition.</li><li>Watch for words with positive or negative charge: <em>undermine</em> is negative, <em>bolster</em> positive.</li><li>Ten words learned properly beat a hundred skimmed.</li><li>Reading serious journalism daily builds this faster than any list.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>Novel</em> usually means a book. In <em>&ldquo;The team proposed a novel approach to flood control&rdquo;</em> it means <strong>new</strong>. The SAT would offer <em>fictional</em> as a trap, because it matches the common meaning rather than the sentence.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Vocabulary in context appears in every Reading &amp; Writing module. Because the words are academic rather than rare, this is one of the most improvable areas on the whole test.</p></div>",
  "Grammar Basics": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Grammar Basics</strong><br><span>A small set of rules produces most of the Standard English Conventions questions.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-foundation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT foundation guide for Grammar Basics\"><figcaption>Grammar Basics: understand the rule and apply it</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Subject</strong><span>The students</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Verb</strong><span>analyze</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Complete thought</strong><span>The students analyze the evidence.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>grammar</summary><p>The system of rules governing how words form meaningful sentences.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>subject</summary><p>The person, thing, or idea a clause is about.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>predicate</summary><p>The part of a clause stating what the subject does or is.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>agreement</summary><p>The grammatical matching of related sentence elements.</p></details></section><p>Roughly a quarter of Reading &amp; Writing is grammar, and it is tested from a short list: subject&ndash;verb agreement, pronouns, verb tense, modifiers, parallel structure, punctuation between clauses, and transitions.</p><p>SAT grammar has right and wrong answers, not stylistic preferences. That makes it the most reliably improvable part of the test: learn the rule, apply it, get the point.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>A <strong>clause</strong> has a subject and a verb; an <strong>independent</strong> clause can stand alone.</li><li>Two independent clauses may not be joined by a comma alone (a comma splice).</li><li>A verb agrees with its <strong>subject</strong>, not with whatever noun sits nearest it.</li><li>A pronoun must agree with its antecedent and refer to it unambiguously.</li><li>Keep tense consistent unless the meaning genuinely requires a change.</li><li>When two answers mean the same thing, the shorter one is usually right.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;The list of required documents were posted.&rdquo;</em> The subject is <strong>list</strong>, singular, not <em>documents</em>. Correct: <em>&ldquo;The list of required documents <strong>was</strong> posted.&rdquo;</em> The prepositional phrase between them is there to mislead you.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Grammar questions are quick and rule-based, so they are the cheapest points on the test. A student who masters this list can pick up 40&ndash;60 scaled points in a fortnight.</p></div>",
  "Sentence Structure": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Sentence Structure</strong><br><span>Almost every punctuation question is really a question about clause boundaries.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-foundation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT foundation guide for Sentence Structure\"><figcaption>Sentence Structure: understand the rule and apply it</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Simple</strong><span>Researchers recorded the change.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Compound</strong><span>They recorded it, and they reported it.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Complex</strong><span>Because the climate changed, migration increased.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>independent clause</summary><p>A word group with a subject and verb that expresses a complete thought.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dependent clause</summary><p>A word group with a subject and verb that cannot stand alone.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>compound sentence</summary><p>Two independent clauses joined correctly.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>complex sentence</summary><p>An independent clause combined with at least one dependent clause.</p></details></section><p>A <strong>simple</strong> sentence is one independent clause. A <strong>compound</strong> sentence joins two independent clauses. A <strong>complex</strong> sentence joins an independent clause to a dependent one. Recognising which you have tells you what punctuation is legal.</p><p>The practical method: cover the punctuation and ask whether each side could stand alone as a sentence. Two standalone sides need a full stop, a semicolon, or a comma plus <em>and, but, or, nor, for, so, yet</em>. One standalone side and one fragment usually take a comma or nothing.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Test each side of the punctuation: <strong>could it stand alone?</strong></li><li>Independent + independent &rarr; period, semicolon, or comma + FANBOYS.</li><li>Dependent + independent &rarr; comma after the dependent part.</li><li>Independent + dependent &rarr; usually no comma.</li><li>A colon must follow a complete sentence, and introduces an explanation or list.</li><li>A dash can work like a comma or a colon, but must be used consistently in a pair.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;The rains arrived late ___ the harvest was poor.&rdquo;</em> Both sides stand alone, so a comma alone is wrong. Legal: a semicolon, a period, or <em>&ldquo;late, so the harvest was poor&rdquo;</em>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Once you test clause boundaries mechanically, punctuation questions stop being about instinct. This single habit is worth several points in every Reading &amp; Writing module.</p></div>",
  "Parts of Speech": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Parts of Speech</strong><br><span>Naming the job a word does is how you spot the error.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-foundation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT foundation guide for Parts of Speech\"><figcaption>Parts of Speech: understand the rule and apply it</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Noun</strong><span>evidence</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Verb</strong><span>supports</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Modifier</strong><span>strong evidence · supports clearly</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>part of speech</summary><p>A category based on the function a word performs.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>noun</summary><p>A word naming a person, place, thing, or idea.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verb</summary><p>A word expressing action, occurrence, or state.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>modifier</summary><p>A word or phrase that describes or limits another element.</p></details></section><p>Nouns name; verbs act; adjectives describe nouns; adverbs describe verbs, adjectives or other adverbs; pronouns replace nouns; prepositions place things in time or space; conjunctions join.</p><p>You are not tested on the labels. You are tested on consequences of them &mdash; whether a describing word should be <em>quick</em> or <em>quickly</em>, whether a pronoun should be <em>who</em> or <em>whom</em>, whether a phrase is describing the right noun.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Adjectives modify nouns; adverbs modify verbs, adjectives and other adverbs.</li><li><em>Good</em> is an adjective, <em>well</em> is the adverb: <em>she writes well</em>.</li><li><em>Who</em> is a subject pronoun, <em>whom</em> an object pronoun.</li><li><em>Fewer</em> is for countable nouns, <em>less</em> for uncountable amounts.</li><li>A preposition is always followed by a noun or noun phrase, never by a clause subject.</li><li>Identify the subject by asking who or what performs the verb.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;The engine runs ___.&rdquo;</em> The word describes <em>runs</em>, a verb, so it must be an adverb: <strong>smoothly</strong>, not <em>smooth</em>. The same logic decides <em>fewer farmers</em> (countable) against <em>less rainfall</em> (uncountable).</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Every grammar rule the SAT tests refers to a part of speech. Being able to name what a word is doing turns a guess into a decision.</p></div>",
  "Punctuation Rules": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Punctuation Rules</strong><br><span>Four marks, one decision each, and no room for taste.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-foundation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT foundation guide for Punctuation Rules\"><figcaption>Punctuation Rules: understand the rule and apply it</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Comma</strong><span>After the survey, researchers compared results.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Semicolon</strong><span>The sample was small; the pattern was clear.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Colon</strong><span>The study measured three traits: height, mass, and speed.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>punctuation</summary><p>Marks used to organize writing and clarify meaning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>comma splice</summary><p>The incorrect joining of independent clauses with only a comma.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>semicolon</summary><p>A mark joining closely related independent clauses.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>colon</summary><p>A mark introducing an explanation, example, or list after a complete clause.</p></details></section><p>The SAT tests commas, semicolons, colons and dashes, plus apostrophes. Each has a narrow legal use, and the test never asks which is prettier &mdash; only which is correct given the clause structure around it.</p><p>The most common trap is punctuation that separates a subject from its verb, or that cuts a sentence where nothing needs cutting. When no rule requires a mark, the answer with no mark is usually right.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Comma</strong>: after an introductory element, around non-essential information, in lists.</li><li><strong>Semicolon</strong>: joins two independent clauses; also separates list items containing commas.</li><li><strong>Colon</strong>: after a complete sentence, introducing an explanation or list.</li><li><strong>Dashes</strong>: interrupt a sentence &mdash; use them in a matched pair.</li><li>Never separate a subject from its verb with a single comma.</li><li><em>Its</em> is possessive; <em>it's</em> means <em>it is</em> or <em>it has</em>.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;The report, which the committee published in March, recommends three changes.&rdquo;</em> The clause is non-essential, so it takes a matched pair of commas. Remove one and the sentence is wrong; remove both and it is also acceptable but changes emphasis.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Punctuation is roughly a fifth of the grammar questions and is entirely rule-driven. Learning four rules properly is faster than any amount of reading practice.</p></div>",
  "Transition Words": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Transition Words</strong><br><span>The right transition is decided by logic, not by how the sentence sounds.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-foundation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT foundation guide for Transition Words\"><figcaption>Transition Words: understand the rule and apply it</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Addition</strong><span>Furthermore</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Contrast</strong><span>However</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Cause/result</strong><span>Therefore</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>transition</summary><p>A word or phrase showing the relationship between ideas.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>logical relationship</summary><p>A connection such as contrast, cause, or continuation.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>concession</summary><p>Acknowledgment of an opposing or limiting point.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>sequence</summary><p>The order in which events or ideas occur.</p></details></section><p>Transition questions give you a blank between two sentences and four connecting words. The method is mechanical: read both sentences, decide the <strong>relationship</strong> between them in your own words, then pick the transition that matches.</p><p>There are only a few relationships to recognise &mdash; contrast, addition, cause, example, sequence, concession, emphasis. Learn which words signal each and the questions become almost automatic.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Contrast</strong>: however, nevertheless, by contrast, on the other hand.</li><li><strong>Addition</strong>: moreover, furthermore, in addition, similarly.</li><li><strong>Cause and effect</strong>: therefore, consequently, thus, as a result.</li><li><strong>Example</strong>: for example, for instance, specifically.</li><li><strong>Concession</strong>: admittedly, granted, to be sure.</li><li>Name the relationship <em>before</em> you look at the four choices.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;Yields rose in the north. ___, they fell sharply in the south.&rdquo;</em> The relationship is contrast, so <strong>However</strong> is right and <em>Therefore</em>, <em>Moreover</em> and <em>For example</em> are all wrong &mdash; regardless of which sounds smoothest.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Transitions appear several times in every Reading &amp; Writing module and are pure logic, so they reward method rather than reading speed.</p></div>",
  "Context Clues": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Context Clues</strong><br><span>The passage always tells you what the word means; the skill is noticing where.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-foundation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT foundation guide for Context Clues\"><figcaption>Context Clues: understand the rule and apply it</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Definition</strong><span>Nocturnal, meaning active at night...</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Contrast</strong><span>Unlike scarce food, abundant food...</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Example</strong><span>Pollinators, such as bees and butterflies...</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>context clue</summary><p>Nearby information that helps reveal a word's meaning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>definition clue</summary><p>A direct explanation placed near an unfamiliar word.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>contrast clue</summary><p>An opposite idea used to clarify meaning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>inference</summary><p>A logical conclusion drawn from textual evidence.</p></details></section><p>When you meet an unfamiliar word, the surrounding sentence usually defines it in one of four ways: by <strong>definition</strong>, by <strong>contrast</strong>, by <strong>example</strong>, or by <strong>cause and effect</strong>. Recognising which clue is present tells you where to look.</p><p>The most reliable habit is to cover the answer choices, predict a simple word of your own that fits the blank, and only then compare it against the options. Predicting first stops the wrong-but-familiar choice from capturing you.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Definition clue</strong>: the meaning is restated after a comma or dash.</li><li><strong>Contrast clue</strong>: <em>but, although, unlike, whereas</em> signal an opposite meaning nearby.</li><li><strong>Example clue</strong>: <em>such as, for instance</em> illustrate the word.</li><li><strong>Cause clue</strong>: <em>because, therefore, so</em> tie the word to a result.</li><li>Predict your own simple word before reading the options.</li><li>Check the charge: does the sentence want something positive or negative?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;Unlike her ebullient brother, Sarah was reserved and quiet.&rdquo;</em> <em>Unlike</em> is a contrast clue, and the contrast is with <em>reserved and quiet</em>, so <strong>ebullient</strong> must mean lively or enthusiastic &mdash; without ever having seen the word before.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Context clues let you answer vocabulary questions containing words you do not know, which is exactly the situation the test is designed to create.</p></div>",
  "Reading Strategies": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Reading Strategies</strong><br><span>On the Digital SAT, read the question first &mdash; the passages are short enough to allow it.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-foundation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT foundation guide for Reading Strategies\"><figcaption>Reading Strategies: understand the rule and apply it</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Question</strong><span>Identify exactly what the item asks.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Text</strong><span>Locate and understand the relevant sentence.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Answer</strong><span>Choose the option fully supported by evidence.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>active reading</summary><p>Purposeful reading focused on structure, claims, and evidence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>evidence</summary><p>Textual information supporting a conclusion.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>paraphrase</summary><p>The same meaning expressed in different words.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>elimination</summary><p>Removing options that conflict with the text or task.</p></details></section><p>Digital SAT passages run about 25&ndash;150 words with a single question each, so the old advice about mapping long passages no longer applies. Read the question, then read the passage knowing what you are looking for.</p><p>Answer in your own words before looking at the options. Then find the choice that matches your answer, rather than judging four choices in isolation &mdash; three of them are written to sound reasonable.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Read the <strong>question first</strong>; each passage serves only one question.</li><li>Answer in your own words, then match to a choice.</li><li>The correct answer is always supported by the text &mdash; you must be able to point at it.</li><li>Eliminate choices that are true in the world but absent from the passage.</li><li>Watch extreme words: <em>always, never, all, only</em> are rarely correct.</li><li>Do not spend more than about 70 seconds on a Reading &amp; Writing question.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A passage describes a study finding a link between soil pH and yield. A choice reading <em>&ldquo;soil pH is the most important factor in crop yield&rdquo;</em> is a trap: the passage showed a link, not primacy. The word <em>most</em> is doing the damage.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Every Reading &amp; Writing question is answerable from its own short passage. Making yourself point at the supporting words is what separates a reliable 650 from an inconsistent 550.</p></div>",
  "Critical Thinking Skills": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Critical Thinking Skills</strong><br><span>The SAT rewards the reader who can tell a claim from the evidence for it.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-foundation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT foundation guide for Critical Thinking Skills\"><figcaption>Critical Thinking Skills: understand the rule and apply it</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Claim</strong><span>What does the author assert?</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>What information supports it?</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reasoning</strong><span>Does the evidence logically justify the claim?</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>critical thinking</summary><p>Careful analysis and evaluation of information before reaching a conclusion.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>claim</summary><p>A statement presented as true or arguable.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>assumption</summary><p>An unstated idea accepted as part of an argument.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>reasoning</summary><p>The logical connection between evidence and a conclusion.</p></details></section><p>Most Reading &amp; Writing questions are really asking one of three things: what is being <strong>claimed</strong>, what <strong>evidence</strong> supports it, and what would <strong>weaken</strong> or strengthen it. Learning to separate those three parts in any passage covers a large share of the section.</p><p>A claim is an assertion. Evidence is data, observation or citation offered for it. An assumption is what must be true for the evidence to support the claim &mdash; and unstated assumptions are where the test hides its hardest questions.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Separate <strong>claim</strong>, <strong>evidence</strong> and <strong>assumption</strong> as you read.</li><li>Correlation is not causation, and the SAT tests that distinction often.</li><li>To weaken an argument, attack its assumption or offer an alternative explanation.</li><li>To strengthen one, rule out an alternative explanation.</li><li>A conclusion cannot be broader than the evidence given for it.</li><li>Beware sample problems: a study of one village does not support a national claim.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A passage reports that students who eat breakfast score higher, and concludes breakfast improves scores. The assumption is that nothing else differs between the groups. A choice noting that breakfast-eaters also sleep more <strong>weakens</strong> it by offering an alternative explanation.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Command-of-evidence and inference questions are the largest question group in Reading &amp; Writing. They test reasoning about text, so they improve through method rather than through reading more.</p></div>",
  "Finding Main Ideas": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Finding Main Ideas</strong><br><span>The main idea is what the whole passage is for, not the most striking sentence in it.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-info-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT information analysis for Finding Main Ideas\"><figcaption>Finding Main Ideas: find evidence and reason carefully</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Topic</strong><span>What is the text mainly about?</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Claim</strong><span>What central point does the author make?</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Best answer</strong><span>Covers the full text without adding information.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>main idea</summary><p>The central point that unifies all important details in a text.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>scope</summary><p>The range of information covered by an answer choice.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>supporting detail</summary><p>A fact or example that develops the main idea.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>summary</summary><p>A brief statement of the text's essential ideas.</p></details></section><p>A main-idea question asks for the <strong>central claim</strong> of a short passage. The trap answers are usually true statements taken from a single sentence &mdash; accurate, but too narrow to be what the passage is doing.</p><p>The reliable method is to summarise the passage in your own words in one short sentence before you look at the options, then reject any choice that is narrower than your summary, wider than it, or about something the passage only mentioned in passing.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>The main idea covers the <strong>whole</strong> passage, not one sentence of it.</li><li>Too narrow is the most common trap: a true detail offered as the main point.</li><li>Too broad is the second: a claim the passage never supports in full.</li><li>The last sentence often states or restates the main idea.</li><li>Summarise in your own words <em>before</em> reading the options.</li><li>If a choice introduces a subject the passage never discusses, eliminate it immediately.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A passage explains that a soil study found three factors affecting yield and that pH mattered most. <em>&ldquo;pH affects crop yield&rdquo;</em> is too narrow; <em>&ldquo;Agriculture depends on soil science&rdquo;</em> is too broad. The main idea is that of the three factors studied, pH proved the most influential.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Main-idea questions appear in every Reading &amp; Writing module, and the same too-narrow / too-broad test also settles many summary and purpose questions.</p></div>",
  "Supporting Details": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Supporting Details</strong><br><span>Details exist to prove the claim &mdash; the question is which one does the proving.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-info-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT information analysis for Supporting Details\"><figcaption>Supporting Details: find evidence and reason carefully</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Claim</strong><span>Urban trees reduce heat.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Measured shade areas were 4°C cooler.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Link</strong><span>The measurement directly supports the claim.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>supporting detail</summary><p>A fact, reason, example, or statistic that develops a central idea.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>evidence</summary><p>Information used to support a conclusion.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>relevance</summary><p>A direct connection between evidence and the claim.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>citation</summary><p>A reference to information taken from a source.</p></details></section><p>A supporting-detail question gives you a claim and asks which piece of evidence from the passage backs it. These are the fastest points in Reading &amp; Writing because the answer is printed in front of you; the skill is matching precisely rather than approximately.</p><p>The Digital SAT also asks the reverse: it presents four pieces of data or quotation and asks which would <strong>most effectively</strong> support a stated conclusion. Here the correct answer must match the conclusion's exact terms, not merely its topic.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Locate the claim first, then hunt for evidence &mdash; never the other way round.</li><li>The right evidence matches the claim's <strong>specific terms</strong>, not just its subject.</li><li>Evidence about a different group, period or measure is wrong however true it sounds.</li><li>If the claim is comparative, the evidence must compare.</li><li>If the claim is causal, evidence of correlation alone is insufficient.</li><li>You should be able to point at the exact words that do the supporting.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Claim: <em>&ldquo;Rainfall in the north exceeded the southern average.&rdquo;</em> The supporting choice must give figures for <strong>both</strong> regions. A choice giving only northern rainfall, however precise, cannot support a comparison.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Command-of-evidence is one of the largest question groups on the test, and unlike inference it has an answer you can literally point to &mdash; making it the most reliable place to gain marks.</p></div>",
  "Making Inferences": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Making Inferences</strong><br><span>One logical step from the text &mdash; never two, and never from your own knowledge.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-info-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT information analysis for Making Inferences\"><figcaption>Making Inferences: find evidence and reason carefully</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>The soil was wet and umbrellas filled the doorway.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reasoning</strong><span>Both clues follow recent rain.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Inference</strong><span>It probably rained shortly before.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>inference</summary><p>A logical conclusion supported by textual evidence but not stated directly.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>imply</summary><p>To suggest an idea without saying it explicitly.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>assumption</summary><p>An unstated belief that may lack adequate evidence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>logical support</summary><p>Evidence and reasoning that justify a conclusion.</p></details></section><p>An inference is a conclusion the passage strongly supports without stating outright. The stem usually says <em>suggests</em>, <em>implies</em>, <em>indicates</em>, or asks which choice most logically completes the text.</p><p>The correct answer is always <strong>one short step</strong> from something printed. Wrong answers fall into three families: they take two steps, they import outside knowledge, or they overstate a qualified claim into an absolute one.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>The answer must be supported by the text and by nothing else.</li><li>One step only. If you need a second assumption, it is the wrong choice.</li><li>Reject absolutes &mdash; <em>always, never, all, only, must</em> &mdash; unless the passage is that strong.</li><li>Reject anything true in the world but absent from the passage.</li><li>For <em>&ldquo;most logically completes&rdquo;</em>, read the sentence before the blank for the logical direction.</li><li>Prove your choice: name the words that force it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Passage: <em>&ldquo;Although the clinic opened in 2015, no vaccination records exist before 2018.&rdquo;</em> Inference: records were <strong>not kept</strong> in those first years. Not <em>&ldquo;the clinic was closed&rdquo;</em> &mdash; that contradicts the text &mdash; and not <em>&ldquo;funding was withdrawn&rdquo;</em>, which the passage never mentions.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Inference questions are where strong readers most often talk themselves out of a correct answer. The discipline of pointing at supporting words is what makes them consistent.</p></div>",
  "Interpreting Data and Graphs": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Interpreting Data and Graphs</strong><br><span>Read the axes before you read the choices.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-info-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT information analysis for Interpreting Data and Graphs\"><figcaption>Interpreting Data and Graphs: find evidence and reason carefully</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Read</strong><span>Identify title, axes, units, and categories.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Compare</strong><span>Calculate the relevant change or difference.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Connect</strong><span>Choose the statement matching both text and graph.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>data interpretation</summary><p>The process of understanding values, patterns, and relationships in data.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>axis</summary><p>A reference line on a graph showing a variable and scale.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>trend</summary><p>A general direction of change across values.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>outlier</summary><p>A value noticeably different from the rest of a dataset.</p></details></section><p>Reading &amp; Writing includes questions pairing a short passage with a table, bar chart, line graph or scatterplot. You are usually asked which choice accurately describes the data, or which completes a claim <em>consistently with</em> the graphic.</p><p>No mathematics is required. What is required is care: read the title, both axis labels, the units and the legend before looking at the answers. Most wrong choices are wrong because they mix up a unit, a category or a direction.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Read title, axis labels, units and legend <strong>first</strong>.</li><li>Check whether values are counts, percentages or rates &mdash; they are not interchangeable.</li><li>A rising percentage can accompany a falling count if the total shrank.</li><li>Answer only from the graphic; do not add what the passage implies unless asked to combine them.</li><li>Watch direction words: <em>increased, exceeded, fewer than, at least</em>.</li><li>When a claim must be consistent with the data, verify the numbers, not the wording.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A bar chart shows cassava output in tonnes; a choice describes the change &ldquo;by 15 percent&rdquo;. Unless the chart shows percentages, that choice cannot be verified from it and is wrong &mdash; even if 15 percent happens to be arithmetically correct.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Data questions appear in both modules of Reading &amp; Writing and are pure accuracy. They reward slow reading of the labels far more than any reasoning skill.</p></div>",
  "Evidence-Based Questions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Evidence-Based Questions</strong><br><span>The SAT wants the evidence that does the work, not the evidence that sounds impressive.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-info-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT information analysis for Evidence-Based Questions\"><figcaption>Evidence-Based Questions: find evidence and reason carefully</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Claim</strong><span>Identify the conclusion the question asks you to support.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Locate</strong><span>Find the exact sentence or data point.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Prove</strong><span>Select evidence that directly establishes the claim.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>evidence-based question</summary><p>A question requiring textual or quantitative proof for a conclusion.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>claim</summary><p>A statement presented for acceptance or evaluation.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>quotation</summary><p>Exact words taken from a source.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>corroborate</summary><p>To strengthen a claim with additional evidence.</p></details></section><p>Command-of-evidence questions come in two forms. <strong>Textual</strong> evidence asks which quotation best supports a claim. <strong>Quantitative</strong> evidence asks which data point from a table or graph does. Both test the same thing: precision of match.</p><p>The test frequently offers evidence that is genuinely relevant to the topic but does not support the specific claim being made. Relevance is not support &mdash; the evidence must address the claim's exact terms.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Restate the claim in your own words before examining the choices.</li><li>Ask of each choice: does this make the claim <strong>more likely to be true</strong>?</li><li>Relevant to the topic is not the same as supporting the claim.</li><li>For a hypothesis, the right evidence usually confirms its specific prediction.</li><li>For quantitative items, verify the numbers against the graphic rather than trusting the wording.</li><li>If two choices seem to support the claim, one addresses it more directly &mdash; find which.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A researcher hypothesises that shade increases seedling survival. The supporting choice must compare shaded and unshaded survival rates. A choice reporting that shaded seedlings grew taller is relevant, interesting, and does not test the hypothesis.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Command-of-evidence is among the most heavily represented question types on the Digital SAT. Because the answer is verifiable rather than interpretive, method reliably beats intuition here.</p></div>",
  "Text Analysis": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Text Analysis</strong><br><span>Ask what the passage is doing, not only what it says.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-info-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT information analysis for Text Analysis\"><figcaption>Text Analysis: find evidence and reason carefully</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Purpose</strong><span>Why did the author include this sentence?</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Method</strong><span>Definition, comparison, example, or cause?</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Effect</strong><span>How does it advance the argument?</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>text analysis</summary><p>Close examination of how a text's ideas, language, and structure create meaning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>rhetorical function</summary><p>The job performed by a sentence or passage.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>context</summary><p>Information surrounding a statement that shapes its meaning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>interpretation</summary><p>An evidence-supported explanation of meaning.</p></details></section><p>Text-analysis questions ask about function: why a sentence is there, how a paragraph is built, what role an example plays. The content matters less than the <strong>job</strong> each part performs.</p><p>Useful labels: a sentence may introduce, define, illustrate, qualify, contrast, concede, or conclude. Naming the job before reading the options makes the right choice obvious.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Ask <em>why is this sentence here?</em> rather than <em>what does it say?</em></li><li>Common jobs: introduce, define, illustrate, qualify, contrast, concede, conclude.</li><li>An example never <em>proves</em> a claim on the SAT &mdash; it illustrates it.</li><li>A concession (<em>admittedly, granted</em>) strengthens the argument that follows it.</li><li>Choices describing the content correctly but the function wrongly are the standard trap.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A passage argues rice yields can rise, then adds: <em>&ldquo;Admittedly, three seasons of data is a short record.&rdquo;</em> Its function is to <strong>acknowledge a limitation</strong>, not to reject the argument. A choice saying it &ldquo;refutes the earlier claim&rdquo; misreads the job entirely.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Function questions look subjective and are not. Once you name the job in your own words, the correct option usually matches it almost word for word.</p></div>",
  "M3:Practice Exercises": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 M3:Practice Exercises</strong><br><span>Practice for Information and Ideas: main idea, detail, inference, data.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-info-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT information analysis for Practice Exercises\"><figcaption>Practice Exercises: find evidence and reason carefully</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Solve</strong><span>Answer one item without timing.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Explain</strong><span>Write why the correct answer works.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Transfer</strong><span>Solve a similar timed item using the same method.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>guided practice</summary><p>Supported application of a newly learned skill.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>worked solution</summary><p>A step-by-step explanation of how an answer is reached.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>error analysis</summary><p>Review of why an answer was wrong and how to correct the method.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>transfer</summary><p>Application of a learned skill to a new problem.</p></details></section><p>This lesson consolidates Module 3. Every question here is answerable from a short passage or a graphic, and every correct answer can be pointed at. Work through them untimed first, then repeat at about <strong>70 seconds per question</strong>.</p><p>Mark each attempt with the reason for any error: too narrow, too broad, outside knowledge, misread unit, or overstated absolute. Those five labels cover almost every mistake made in this module.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Main idea &mdash; whole passage, not one sentence.</li><li>Supporting detail &mdash; the evidence must match the claim's exact terms.</li><li>Inference &mdash; one step from the text, nothing imported.</li><li>Data &mdash; read title, axes, units and legend before the choices.</li><li>Log every error by <em>reason</em>, not by topic.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">After ten practice questions a student finds six errors: four <em>too narrow</em> and two <em>misread unit</em>. That is not a reading problem, it is two habits. The next session drills summarising before looking at choices, and reading axis labels aloud.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Practice only improves a score when it is reviewed. Sorting errors by reason turns a score out of ten into a specific plan for the next session.</p></div>",
  "Vocabulary in Context": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Vocabulary in Context</strong><br><span>The sentence decides the meaning, not the dictionary.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-craft-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT craft and structure guide for Vocabulary in Context\"><figcaption>Vocabulary in Context: examine language, purpose and structure</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Read</strong><span>Study the sentence and nearby contrast or example clues.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Substitute</strong><span>Insert each option without changing grammar.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Choose</strong><span>Preserve the exact meaning and tone.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>contextual meaning</summary><p>The meaning a word has in a particular sentence or passage.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>connotation</summary><p>A feeling or association connected to a word.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>substitution</summary><p>Replacing one expression with another to test meaning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>precision</summary><p>Exactness in meaning and word choice.</p></details></section><p>These questions give a short passage with a blank or an underlined word and ask which choice best fits the meaning <em>as used</em>. Many tested words are common words carrying an academic sense.</p><p>Cover the choices. Read the sentence and put your own simple word in the gap. Then find the choice closest to your word. Doing it in this order stops the familiar-but-wrong option from capturing you.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Predict your own word <strong>before</strong> reading the four choices.</li><li>The common meaning of a word is usually the trap, not the answer.</li><li>Match the sentence's <em>charge</em>: positive, negative or neutral.</li><li>Substitute your chosen word back in and re-read the whole sentence.</li><li>If two choices both fit loosely, one fits the register of academic writing better.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;The findings <strong>qualify</strong> earlier conclusions about migration.&rdquo;</em> Not <em>&ldquo;make eligible&rdquo;</em>, the common meaning. Here it means <strong>limit or moderate</strong>. Predicting &ldquo;soften&rdquo; or &ldquo;restrict&rdquo; leads you straight to it.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Vocabulary in context appears several times in every module, and because it rewards a repeatable method rather than a memorised list, it is one of the fastest areas to improve.</p></div>",
  "Author's Purpose": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Author's Purpose</strong><br><span>Every passage is written to do something: inform, argue, describe, or persuade.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-craft-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT craft and structure guide for Author's Purpose\"><figcaption>Author's Purpose: examine language, purpose and structure</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Text choice</strong><span>What did the author include?</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Function</strong><span>To explain, support, contrast, or qualify?</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Purpose</strong><span>State what the choice helps the author accomplish.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>author's purpose</summary><p>The reason an author creates a text or includes a particular element.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>rhetorical function</summary><p>The role a sentence or detail performs.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>audience</summary><p>The intended readers of a text.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>qualification</summary><p>A limit or condition added to make a claim more accurate.</p></details></section><p>Purpose questions ask why the author wrote the passage or a particular part of it. The four broad purposes are to <strong>inform</strong>, to <strong>argue</strong>, to <strong>describe</strong> and to <strong>persuade</strong>, and the wording of the passage usually announces which.</p><p>Neutral, factual language signals informing. Evaluative language &mdash; <em>should, must, fails to, compelling</em> &mdash; signals arguing or persuading. Sensory and concrete language signals describing.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Neutral factual language &rarr; informing.</li><li>Evaluative or prescriptive language &rarr; arguing or persuading.</li><li>Concrete, sensory language &rarr; describing.</li><li>A purpose choice must cover the whole passage, not one striking sentence.</li><li>Watch verbs in the choices: <em>refute</em>, <em>illustrate</em>, <em>summarise</em> and <em>advocate</em> are not interchangeable.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A passage lists three irrigation methods with their costs and yields, using no evaluative language. Its purpose is to <strong>inform</strong> or compare. A choice saying it &ldquo;advocates drip irrigation&rdquo; requires recommending language the passage never uses.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Purpose questions test whether you register tone as well as content, and they use the same too-narrow / too-broad logic that settles main-idea questions.</p></div>",
  "Tone and Style": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Tone and Style</strong><br><span>Tone is the author's attitude; style is how the writing is built.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-craft-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT craft and structure guide for Tone and Style\"><figcaption>Tone and Style: examine language, purpose and structure</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Words</strong><span>Notice positive, negative, formal, or cautious language.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Pattern</strong><span>Observe sentence length and level of certainty.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Conclusion</strong><span>Name the attitude with precise evidence.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>tone</summary><p>The author's attitude toward the subject or audience.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>style</summary><p>The characteristic way an author uses language.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>diction</summary><p>The author's choice of words.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>formality</summary><p>The degree to which language follows conventional academic standards.</p></details></section><p><strong>Tone</strong> is attitude toward the subject: approving, critical, sceptical, admiring, neutral, cautious. <strong>Style</strong> is the manner: formal or informal, technical or plain, dense or direct.</p><p>Academic writing on the SAT is rarely emotional. Extreme tone words &mdash; <em>furious, ecstatic, contemptuous</em> &mdash; are almost always wrong. The right answer is usually a measured word such as <em>cautious</em>, <em>appreciative</em>, <em>sceptical</em> or <em>objective</em>.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Tone = attitude; style = manner of expression.</li><li>Extreme emotional words are almost always the wrong choice.</li><li>Adjectives and adverbs carry tone; nouns and verbs mostly carry content.</li><li>A hedge (<em>may, appears, suggests</em>) signals a cautious tone.</li><li>In Expression of Ideas, keep the style of a revision consistent with the passage's.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;The results are promising, though the sample was small and further work is needed.&rdquo;</em> The tone is <strong>cautiously optimistic</strong> &mdash; not <em>enthusiastic</em>, which ignores the hedging, and not <em>dismissive</em>, which ignores <em>promising</em>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Tone questions are quick when you look at the modifiers rather than the subject matter, and the same instinct stops you choosing an over-formal or over-casual revision in Expression of Ideas.</p></div>",
  "Comparing Passages": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Comparing Passages</strong><br><span>Two texts, one question: where exactly do they disagree?</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-craft-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT craft and structure guide for Comparing Passages\"><figcaption>Comparing Passages: examine language, purpose and structure</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Passage 1</strong><span>Record its claim and evidence.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Passage 2</strong><span>Record agreement, disagreement, or extension.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Relationship</strong><span>Choose the option describing both accurately.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>paired texts</summary><p>Two passages examined together because their ideas are related.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>agreement</summary><p>A point on which authors share a position.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>contrast</summary><p>A meaningful difference between ideas or methods.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>perspective</summary><p>A particular viewpoint from which a subject is understood.</p></details></section><p>Paired-passage questions give two short texts on the same subject and ask how the second relates to the first. The usual relationships are agreement, disagreement, qualification, or applying the first idea to a new case.</p><p>Summarise each text's position in your own words before reading the choices. Most trap answers describe one text correctly and the other wrongly, so check both halves of every option.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Summarise each text separately before comparing them.</li><li>Common relationships: agrees, disagrees, qualifies, extends, applies.</li><li>Check <strong>both halves</strong> of a choice &mdash; one half is often deliberately wrong.</li><li>Disagreement is usually about a specific point, not the whole subject.</li><li>Watch for a second text that accepts the finding but rejects the explanation.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Text 1 argues drought caused a yield fall. Text 2 accepts the fall but attributes it to poor seed. They agree on the <strong>fact</strong> and disagree on the <strong>cause</strong> &mdash; a choice saying &ldquo;Text 2 disputes Text 1's data&rdquo; is wrong.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Paired passages look like double the work and are not, provided you fix each position in your own words first. The comparison then takes seconds.</p></div>",
  "Text Structure": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Text Structure</strong><br><span>How a passage is organised is itself a testable fact.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-craft-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT craft and structure guide for Text Structure\"><figcaption>Text Structure: examine language, purpose and structure</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Sequence</strong><span>Ideas unfold by time or steps.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Cause/effect</strong><span>One condition produces a result.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Compare/contrast</strong><span>Similarities and differences organize the text.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>text structure</summary><p>The organizational pattern used to arrange ideas.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>chronology</summary><p>Organization according to time order.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>cause and effect</summary><p>A structure explaining why something happens and what follows.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>problem and solution</summary><p>A structure presenting a difficulty and a response.</p></details></section><p>Structure questions ask how a passage is arranged: problem then solution, claim then evidence, chronological, general then specific, cause then effect, or comparison. Short SAT passages usually follow one clean pattern.</p><p>Read the first and last sentences and ask what changed between them. That movement &mdash; from question to answer, from general to particular, from past to present &mdash; is the structure.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Common patterns: problem/solution, claim/evidence, chronological, general/specific, cause/effect, compare/contrast.</li><li>Compare the first and last sentences to see the movement.</li><li>Signal words mark the pattern: <em>first, then, however, as a result, for instance</em>.</li><li>A choice must describe the whole arrangement, not one transition inside it.</li><li>In Expression of Ideas, structure decides where a new sentence should be inserted.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A passage opens with falling fish stocks and closes with a proposed quota system. The structure is <strong>problem then solution</strong>. A choice calling it &ldquo;a chronological account&rdquo; describes the surface, not the arrangement.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Recognising structure answers structure questions directly and also tells you where an inserted sentence belongs &mdash; a common Expression of Ideas task.</p></div>",
  "Analyzing Arguments": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Analyzing Arguments</strong><br><span>Find the conclusion first; everything else is either support for it or noise.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-craft-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT craft and structure guide for Analyzing Arguments\"><figcaption>Analyzing Arguments: examine language, purpose and structure</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Claim</strong><span>What conclusion does the author defend?</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Which facts or examples support it?</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reasoning</strong><span>Does the evidence logically justify the claim?</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>argument</summary><p>A claim supported by evidence and reasoning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>premise</summary><p>A reason or statement offered in support of a conclusion.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>validity</summary><p>The quality of reasoning in which a conclusion follows logically.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>counterargument</summary><p>A reasoned position opposing the main claim.</p></details></section><p>An argument has a <strong>conclusion</strong> (what the author wants you to accept), <strong>premises</strong> (the reasons given), and <strong>assumptions</strong> (unstated links the reasoning depends on).</p><p>Locate the conclusion by asking what the passage is trying to convince you of. Words such as <em>therefore, thus, so, it follows that</em> mark it, but often it simply sits in the last sentence.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Conclusion = the claim being urged; premises = the reasons offered.</li><li>An assumption is unstated and necessary &mdash; remove it and the argument collapses.</li><li>To <strong>weaken</strong>: attack the assumption or supply an alternative explanation.</li><li>To <strong>strengthen</strong>: rule out an alternative explanation.</li><li>A conclusion drawn from a small or unrepresentative sample is vulnerable to that fact.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Argument: <em>&ldquo;Schools with libraries score higher, so building libraries will raise scores.&rdquo;</em> The assumption is that the library causes the difference. Noting that such schools are also better funded overall <strong>weakens</strong> it by supplying another explanation.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Argument questions appear throughout Reading &amp; Writing and reappear in Craft and Structure. The conclusion/premise/assumption split handles nearly all of them.</p></div>",
  "M4:Practice Exercises": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 M4:Practice Exercises</strong><br><span>Practice for Craft and Structure: vocabulary, purpose, tone, structure, arguments.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-craft-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT craft and structure guide for Practice Exercises\"><figcaption>Practice Exercises: examine language, purpose and structure</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Attempt</strong><span>Solve a mixed Craft and Structure item.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Justify</strong><span>Point to the word, sentence, or relationship proving the answer.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Review</strong><span>Name the distractor pattern and correct it.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>mixed practice</summary><p>Exercises combining several skills in one set.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>justification</summary><p>A clear reason supported by evidence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>distractor pattern</summary><p>A recurring way an incorrect option misleads test takers.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>reflection</summary><p>Deliberate review of performance to improve future decisions.</p></details></section><p>This lesson consolidates Module 4. These questions reward naming things before choosing: name the word you expect, the purpose, the tone, the structure, or the conclusion &mdash; then match a choice to your name.</p><p>Work untimed first, then repeat at about <strong>70 seconds per question</strong>. Log errors by reason, not by topic.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Vocabulary &mdash; predict your own word first.</li><li>Purpose &mdash; neutral language informs, evaluative language argues.</li><li>Tone &mdash; look at adjectives and adverbs, and avoid extremes.</li><li>Structure &mdash; compare first and last sentences.</li><li>Arguments &mdash; find the conclusion, then the assumption holding it up.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A student misses four questions: two tone (both chose an extreme word) and two purpose (both chose &ldquo;advocates&rdquo; for informative passages). One habit fixes both &mdash; check the modifiers before choosing.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Module 4 questions look interpretive but are decided by textual signals. Practising the naming step is what converts them from guesswork into method.</p></div>",
  "Improving Sentences": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Improving Sentences</strong><br><span>Between two correct options, the SAT always prefers the shorter one.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-expression-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Improving Sentences\"><figcaption>Improving Sentences: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Meaning</strong><span>Preserve the author's intended point.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Grammar</strong><span>Correct agreement, form, and punctuation.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Style</strong><span>Choose the clearest and most concise version.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>revision</summary><p>A change that improves meaning, organization, or language.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>conciseness</summary><p>Clear expression without unnecessary words.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>redundancy</summary><p>Needless repetition of the same idea.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>clarity</summary><p>The quality of being easy to understand.</p></details></section><p>These questions offer four versions of a sentence and ask which is best. First eliminate anything grammatically wrong. Among what survives, choose the version that is clearest and most concise.</p><p>The SAT dislikes redundancy (<em>&ldquo;annual yearly report&rdquo;</em>), wordiness (<em>&ldquo;due to the fact that&rdquo;</em> for <em>because</em>), and passive voice where an active sentence would do.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Eliminate grammatical errors first, then judge concision.</li><li>Cut redundancy: <em>free gift, past history, annual yearly</em>.</li><li>Replace wordy phrases: <em>due to the fact that</em> &rarr; <em>because</em>.</li><li>Prefer active voice unless the doer is genuinely unimportant.</li><li>Never change the meaning to make a sentence shorter.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;Due to the fact that the rains were late, the harvest was, as a result, poor.&rdquo;</em> Two wordy phrases and a doubled causal link. Better: <em>&ldquo;Because the rains were late, the harvest was poor.&rdquo;</em></div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Concision questions are quick and rule-driven. Once you know the SAT's preference, the shortest grammatical option is right often enough to be a reliable tie-breaker.</p></div>",
  "Organization of Paragraphs": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Organization of Paragraphs</strong><br><span>One idea per paragraph, announced in the first sentence.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-expression-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Organization of Paragraphs\"><figcaption>Organization of Paragraphs: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Topic sentence</strong><span>States the paragraph's controlling idea.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Development</strong><span>Adds evidence, examples, and explanation.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Closing</strong><span>Completes the point or links forward.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>paragraph organization</summary><p>The logical arrangement of sentences around one controlling idea.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>topic sentence</summary><p>A sentence stating a paragraph's main point.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>unity</summary><p>The quality of every sentence supporting the same focus.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>development</summary><p>Supporting a central idea with sufficient explanation and evidence.</p></details></section><p>Organisation questions ask where a sentence belongs, which sentence should open a paragraph, or whether a sentence should be kept or removed. All three depend on one principle: a paragraph develops a single idea.</p><p>A topic sentence states that idea. Every other sentence in the paragraph must serve it. A sentence that introduces a different subject belongs elsewhere &mdash; or nowhere.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Each paragraph develops <strong>one</strong> idea, stated in its topic sentence.</li><li>To place a sentence, find the paragraph whose idea it serves.</li><li>Pronouns and <em>this/these</em> point backwards &mdash; use them to locate position.</li><li>A sentence that changes the subject should be removed, however interesting.</li><li>Chronological or logical order within a paragraph is rarely accidental.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A paragraph about irrigation costs contains a sentence about pest resistance. Interesting, well written, and off-topic &mdash; the SAT's answer is to <strong>remove</strong> it, because it does not serve the paragraph's single idea.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Organisation questions are decided by a rule rather than by taste, and the same one-idea principle answers insert, order and delete questions alike.</p></div>",
  "Logical Flow": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Logical Flow</strong><br><span>Ideas must arrive in an order the reader can follow without backtracking.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-expression-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Logical Flow\"><figcaption>Logical Flow: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Given information</strong><span>Begin with what the reader already knows.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>New information</strong><span>Add the next connected idea.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Transition</strong><span>Signal the exact relationship.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>logical flow</summary><p>The clear, reasonable progression of ideas.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>coherence</summary><p>The overall logical connection among ideas.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>transition</summary><p>A word or phrase showing an idea relationship.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>sequence</summary><p>The order in which information is presented.</p></details></section><p>Flow questions test sequence: does this sentence follow from the last one, and does the next follow from it? Problems appear when an idea arrives before the information needed to understand it.</p><p>The most reliable check is to read the sentence before the blank and the sentence after it, ignoring everything else. The gap between those two is what the answer must bridge.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Read only the sentence before and the sentence after the blank.</li><li>A term must be introduced before it is discussed.</li><li>General statements normally precede their specific examples.</li><li>Cause precedes effect unless a transition signals otherwise.</li><li>If a choice repeats what has just been said, it adds nothing and is wrong.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A paragraph mentions <em>&ldquo;this technique&rdquo;</em> in its second sentence, but no technique has been named. The missing first sentence must <strong>introduce the technique</strong> &mdash; not describe its results, which the paragraph goes on to do anyway.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Flow questions are quick once you stop re-reading the whole paragraph. The two neighbouring sentences contain everything the answer must satisfy.</p></div>",
  "Combining Sentences": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Combining Sentences</strong><br><span>The right join is the one that shows the true relationship between the two ideas.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-expression-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Combining Sentences\"><figcaption>Combining Sentences: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Identify</strong><span>Find repeated information and the main relationship.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Combine</strong><span>Use coordination, subordination, or a modifier.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Check</strong><span>Preserve all meaning without creating an error.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>coordination</summary><p>Joining grammatically equal ideas.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>subordination</summary><p>Making one idea dependent on a more important idea.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>relative clause</summary><p>A clause beginning with who, which, or that and describing a noun.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>sentence boundary</summary><p>The punctuation or conjunction separating complete clauses.</p></details></section><p>Sentence-combining questions give you two short sentences and ask for the most effective single sentence. The best answer keeps all the information, removes repetition, and makes the logical relationship explicit.</p><p>Choose the joining method by relationship: a relative clause for added description, a subordinator for cause or contrast, a coordinating conjunction for equal weight, an appositive for renaming.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Keep every piece of information; losing one makes a choice wrong.</li><li>Relative clause (<em>which, who</em>) &mdash; adds description.</li><li>Subordinator (<em>because, although, while</em>) &mdash; shows cause or contrast.</li><li>Appositive &mdash; renames a noun: <em>Musu, a rice farmer, ...</em></li><li>Prefer the version that is both complete and shortest.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;Musu farms rice. She has worked the same plot for twenty years.&rdquo;</em> Combined with an appositive-style relative clause: <em>&ldquo;Musu, who has farmed the same rice plot for twenty years, ...&rdquo;</em> &mdash; one sentence, nothing lost, repetition removed.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Combining questions test the same clause knowledge as punctuation questions, so learning one strengthens the other.</p></div>",
  "Revising Text": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Revising Text</strong><br><span>Every revision question has a stated goal &mdash; answer that goal, not your own taste.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-expression-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Revising Text\"><figcaption>Revising Text: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Purpose</strong><span>Identify what the paragraph must accomplish.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Add, remove, or rearrange material.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Polish</strong><span>Improve transitions, precision, and concision.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>text revision</summary><p>Improving a draft's ideas, evidence, organization, and language.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>relevance</summary><p>Direct connection to the text's purpose.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>reordering</summary><p>Moving information to create clearer progression.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>synthesis</summary><p>Combining information into a coherent whole.</p></details></section><p>Revision questions usually name their purpose: <em>&ldquo;which choice most effectively emphasises the study's limitations?&rdquo;</em> The correct answer is the one that achieves <strong>that stated goal</strong>, even if another option is better written.</p><p>Underline the goal in the question before reading the choices. Then test each option against it alone. A beautifully phrased choice that does not meet the goal is wrong.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Find and hold the <strong>stated goal</strong> in the question stem.</li><li>Test each choice against the goal, not against style.</li><li>A choice may be well written and still fail the goal &mdash; that is the standard trap.</li><li>If the goal is to emphasise, the answer must be specific and pointed.</li><li>If the goal is to introduce, the answer must be general enough to open the topic.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Goal: <em>&ldquo;emphasise how quickly the flooding spread&rdquo;</em>. <em>&ldquo;The water rose across four districts within six hours&rdquo;</em> meets it. <em>&ldquo;The flooding caused considerable disruption&rdquo;</em> is true, fluent, and says nothing about speed.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Goal-directed revision questions are common in Expression of Ideas and are among the easiest to get wrong by answering a question that was not asked.</p></div>",
  "Practice Questions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Practice Questions</strong><br><span>Practice for Expression of Ideas: concision, organisation, flow, goals.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-expression-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Practice Questions\"><figcaption>Practice Questions: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Solve</strong><span>Apply one Expression of Ideas skill.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Explain</strong><span>State why the answer improves the text.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Correct</strong><span>Record the tested rule and retry a similar item.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>practice question</summary><p>A learning item used to apply a tested skill before formal assessment.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>rationale</summary><p>The reason an answer is correct or incorrect.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>error log</summary><p>A record of mistakes, causes, and corrections.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>transfer</summary><p>Use of a learned method in a new context.</p></details></section><p>This lesson consolidates Module 5. Expression of Ideas questions almost always have a stated goal or a structural rule behind them, so the work is to identify which one applies before comparing choices.</p><p>Do these untimed first, then at about <strong>70 seconds each</strong>. Record every error as one of: missed the stated goal, kept redundancy, broke paragraph unity, or misjudged flow.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Concision &mdash; shortest grammatical version that keeps the meaning.</li><li>Organisation &mdash; one idea per paragraph.</li><li>Flow &mdash; the two neighbouring sentences define the gap.</li><li>Goals &mdash; answer the question actually asked.</li><li>Log errors by reason so the next session has a target.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A student misses three: all three were goal questions where a fluent but off-goal choice won. The fix is mechanical &mdash; underline the goal before looking at any option.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Expression of Ideas is the most improvable part of Reading &amp; Writing because almost every question is decided by a rule you can learn rather than by reading ability.</p></div>",
  "Subject-Verb Agreement": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Subject-Verb Agreement</strong><br><span>Find the subject, ignore everything between it and the verb.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-conventions-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Subject-Verb Agreement\"><figcaption>Subject-Verb Agreement: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Singular</strong><span>The collection of poems is valuable.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Plural</strong><span>The poems in the collection are valuable.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Check</strong><span>Ignore interrupting phrases and match the true subject.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>subject-verb agreement</summary><p>The grammatical matching of a subject and verb in number.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>subject</summary><p>The noun or pronoun a clause is about.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>intervening phrase</summary><p>Words between a subject and verb that do not control agreement.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>collective noun</summary><p>A singular noun naming a group.</p></details></section><p>A verb agrees with its subject in number. The SAT hides the subject by inserting phrases between it and the verb, so the noun nearest the verb is usually the wrong one to match.</p><p>Cross out prepositional phrases (<em>of the documents</em>, <em>in the villages</em>) and clauses set off by commas. What remains beside the verb is the subject.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Cross out phrases between the subject and the verb before deciding.</li><li><em>Each, every, either, neither, one, none</em> take a <strong>singular</strong> verb.</li><li>Compound subjects joined by <em>and</em> are plural.</li><li>With <em>or</em> / <em>nor</em>, the verb agrees with the nearer subject.</li><li>In <em>there is</em> / <em>there are</em>, the subject follows the verb.</li><li>Collective nouns (<em>team, committee</em>) usually take a singular verb.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;Each of the twelve districts ___ submitted a report.&rdquo;</em> Cross out <em>of the twelve districts</em>: the subject is <strong>Each</strong>, singular, so <em>has</em>. The plural <em>districts</em> sitting next to the verb is the trap.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Agreement is one of the most frequently tested conventions and one of the easiest to secure, because the crossing-out method works every time.</p></div>",
  "Verb Tenses": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Verb Tenses</strong><br><span>Keep the tense the passage has already established unless meaning demands a change.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-conventions-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Verb Tenses\"><figcaption>Verb Tenses: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Past</strong><span>Researchers observed the pattern.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Present perfect</strong><span>They have observed it repeatedly.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Consistency</strong><span>Shift tense only when time changes.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verb tense</summary><p>A verb form locating an action or state in time.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>aspect</summary><p>A form showing whether an action is ongoing or completed.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>tense shift</summary><p>A change in verb time, correct only when meaning requires it.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>past participle</summary><p>A verb form used with have or be.</p></details></section><p>Most tense questions test <strong>consistency</strong>: a passage written in the past stays in the past. Look at the verbs in the surrounding sentences and match them.</p><p>The perfect tenses appear when one action precedes another. Use the past perfect (<em>had studied</em>) for the earlier of two past actions, and the present perfect (<em>has studied</em>) for something begun in the past and still relevant now.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Match the tense already established in the surrounding sentences.</li><li><strong>Past perfect</strong> (<em>had done</em>) marks the earlier of two past events.</li><li><strong>Present perfect</strong> (<em>has done</em>) links past action to the present.</li><li>A time marker (<em>in 1998, since 2010, currently</em>) fixes the required tense.</li><li>Do not shift tense mid-sentence without a reason in the meaning.</li><li>Scientific findings and permanent truths take the present tense.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;By the time the survey began, the clinic ___ operating for three years.&rdquo;</em> Two past events, and the operating came first, so the past perfect: <strong>had been</strong>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Tense questions are settled by evidence inside the passage &mdash; the surrounding verbs and any time marker &mdash; so they never require guessing.</p></div>",
  "Pronouns": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Pronouns</strong><br><span>A pronoun needs a clear, singular-or-plural-matching antecedent.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-conventions-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Pronouns\"><figcaption>Pronouns: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Antecedent</strong><span>Each sculpture has a label.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Pronoun</strong><span>It identifies the artist.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Check</strong><span>Meaning and number must be clear.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>pronoun</summary><p>A word used in place of a noun or noun phrase.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>antecedent</summary><p>The noun or phrase to which a pronoun refers.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>agreement</summary><p>The matching of a pronoun and antecedent.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>ambiguity</summary><p>Uncertainty caused when a pronoun could refer to more than one noun.</p></details></section><p>Pronoun questions test three things: <strong>agreement</strong> in number with the noun replaced, <strong>case</strong> (subject or object), and <strong>clarity</strong> of reference.</p><p>Ambiguity is the subtlest of the three. If a pronoun could refer to two different nouns, the SAT treats the sentence as wrong even though it is grammatical.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>A pronoun matches its antecedent in number: a singular noun takes <em>it</em>, not <em>they</em>.</li><li>Subject case: <em>I, he, she, we, they, who</em>. Object case: <em>me, him, her, us, them, whom</em>.</li><li>Test <em>who/whom</em> by substituting <em>he/him</em>: if <em>him</em> fits, use <em>whom</em>.</li><li>If a pronoun could point to two nouns, the reference is ambiguous and wrong.</li><li><em>Its</em> is possessive; <em>it's</em> means <em>it is</em>.</li><li><em>Their</em> with a singular indefinite pronoun is not accepted on the SAT.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;When the committee met the researchers, they presented the findings.&rdquo;</em> Who presented &mdash; committee or researchers? The pronoun is <strong>ambiguous</strong>, so the sentence must be rewritten to name the group.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Pronoun errors are common in every module and are easy to miss because ambiguous sentences read perfectly well aloud.</p></div>",
  "Modifiers": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Modifiers</strong><br><span>A modifier attaches to whatever sits next to it &mdash; make sure that is the right thing.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-conventions-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Modifiers\"><figcaption>Modifiers: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Correct</strong><span>Walking through the lab, the scientist took notes.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Dangling</strong><span>Walking through the lab, the notes were taken.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Fix</strong><span>Place the described word beside the modifier.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>modifier</summary><p>A word, phrase, or clause that describes another element.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dangling modifier</summary><p>A modifier whose intended subject is missing.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>misplaced modifier</summary><p>A modifier positioned so that it appears to describe the wrong word.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>adverbial phrase</summary><p>A phrase describing how, when, where, or why.</p></details></section><p>A <strong>misplaced modifier</strong> sits beside the wrong word. A <strong>dangling modifier</strong> has nothing in the sentence to attach to at all. Both produce sentences that are grammatically parseable and factually absurd.</p><p>The test: when a sentence opens with a descriptive phrase followed by a comma, the noun immediately after the comma must be the thing that phrase describes.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>An opening descriptive phrase describes the noun right after the comma.</li><li>A dangling modifier has no noun in the sentence to attach to.</li><li>Place limiting words (<em>only, almost, just</em>) directly before what they limit.</li><li>Move the modifier, or change the subject &mdash; either can fix the sentence.</li><li>Read the resulting sentence literally; if it is absurd, the modifier is wrong.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;Walking through the market, the prices seemed high.&rdquo;</em> Literally, the prices are walking. Fix by supplying the right subject: <em>&ldquo;Walking through the market, <strong>I</strong> found the prices high.&rdquo;</em></div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Modifier questions are among the most reliably identifiable on the test: the opening-phrase-plus-comma pattern tells you exactly what to check.</p></div>",
  "Parallel Structure": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Parallel Structure</strong><br><span>Items in a list or comparison must share the same grammatical form.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-conventions-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Parallel Structure\"><figcaption>Parallel Structure: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Correct</strong><span>The course requires reading, writing, and revising.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Incorrect</strong><span>The course requires reading, to write, and revision.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Pattern</strong><span>Match grammatical forms in equal items.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>parallel structure</summary><p>The use of matching grammatical forms for equal ideas.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>series</summary><p>Three or more items presented in a list.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>correlative conjunction</summary><p>A paired connector such as either...or.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>comparison</summary><p>A structure showing similarity or difference.</p></details></section><p>When a sentence lists items or draws a comparison, every element must take the same form: all nouns, all <em>-ing</em> forms, all infinitives, all clauses.</p><p>Parallelism also governs paired constructions &mdash; <em>not only&hellip;but also</em>, <em>either&hellip;or</em>, <em>both&hellip;and</em>. Whatever follows the first half must be matched in form by what follows the second.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>All items in a list share one grammatical form.</li><li>Paired constructions must be balanced on both sides.</li><li>Comparisons must compare like with like, not a thing with a person's version of it.</li><li>Repeat the preposition or article across items if you repeat it for any of them.</li><li>Read the sentence stem separately with each list item to test it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;She enjoys reading, writing and to swim.&rdquo;</em> Two <em>-ing</em> forms and an infinitive. Correct: <em>&ldquo;reading, writing and <strong>swimming</strong>&rdquo;</em>. Likewise <em>&ldquo;The climate here is milder than Ghana&rdquo;</em> compares a climate with a country &mdash; it should be <em>than Ghana's</em>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Parallelism appears in most Reading &amp; Writing modules and is instantly checkable by reading the stem against each item in turn.</p></div>",
  "Punctuation": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Punctuation</strong><br><span>The mark you need is decided by what sits on each side of it.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-conventions-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Punctuation\"><figcaption>Punctuation: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Comma</strong><span>After the experiment, the team met.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Semicolon</strong><span>The sample was small; the result was useful.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Colon</strong><span>The team needed one item: evidence.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>independent clause</summary><p>A clause that can stand as a complete sentence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>comma splice</summary><p>Two independent clauses joined incorrectly with only a comma.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>semicolon</summary><p>A mark joining closely related independent clauses.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>colon</summary><p>A mark introducing an explanation or list after a complete clause.</p></details></section><p>Module 2 introduced the four marks; this lesson applies them under exam conditions. The single question to ask at every punctuation decision is whether each side of the mark could stand alone as a sentence.</p><p>The SAT also tests <strong>no punctuation</strong> as an answer. When no rule requires a mark, inserting one is an error &mdash; particularly between a subject and its verb, or between a verb and its object.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Independent + independent &rarr; period, semicolon, or comma + FANBOYS.</li><li>Dependent first &rarr; comma after it. Dependent last &rarr; usually no comma.</li><li>Colon after a complete sentence only, introducing an explanation or list.</li><li>Dashes in matched pairs when they enclose an interruption.</li><li>Never place a single comma between a subject and its verb.</li><li>If no rule requires a mark, the answer with no mark is correct.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;The farmers who attended the training ___ reported higher yields.&rdquo;</em> The clause is essential and no rule calls for a mark, so the answer is <strong>no punctuation</strong>. A comma here would cut the subject from its verb.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Punctuation is the largest single group within Standard English Conventions, and every question yields to the same clause test.</p></div>",
  "Capitalization": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Capitalization</strong><br><span>Capitalise proper nouns and sentence openings &mdash; not words you think are important.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-conventions-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Capitalization\"><figcaption>Capitalization: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Proper noun</strong><span>Liberia · College Board · Monday</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Title</strong><span>The History of Science</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Sentence</strong><span>Capitalize the first word after terminal punctuation.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>capitalization</summary><p>The use of uppercase letters according to standard conventions.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>proper noun</summary><p>The specific name of a person, place, organization, or title.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>common noun</summary><p>A general name that normally is not capitalized.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>title case</summary><p>A system for capitalizing principal words in a title.</p></details></section><p>Capitalisation is lightly tested but easy to secure. Capitalise the first word of a sentence, proper nouns and adjectives formed from them, titles used before a name, and the main words of published titles.</p><p>Do not capitalise seasons, directions used generally, job titles used generically, or academic subjects other than languages.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Proper nouns and proper adjectives: <em>Liberia, Liberian, Monrovia</em>.</li><li>Titles before a name are capitalised: <em>President Weah</em>; after, generally not.</li><li>Seasons are lower case: <em>the rains came late in summer</em>.</li><li>Directions are lower case unless they name a region: <em>drive north</em> vs <em>the North</em>.</li><li>Academic subjects are lower case except languages: <em>biology</em> but <em>English</em>.</li><li>Do not capitalise for emphasis &mdash; the SAT never rewards it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;She studied Biology and english at the University in monrovia.&rdquo;</em> Corrected: <em>&ldquo;She studied biology and English at the university in Monrovia.&rdquo;</em> &mdash; language capitalised, subject not, place capitalised, generic noun not.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>These questions take seconds and are decided by a short list of rules, making them among the cheapest points available.</p></div>",
  "Word Choice": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Word Choice</strong><br><span>Commonly confused words are tested by meaning, not by sound.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-conventions-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Word Choice\"><figcaption>Word Choice: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Meaning</strong><span>Choose the word that expresses the exact idea.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Usage</strong><span>Check grammar and natural combinations.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Tone</strong><span>Match formal academic context.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>word choice</summary><p>The selection of language for exact meaning, grammar, and tone.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>idiom</summary><p>A conventional expression whose wording is fixed.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>collocation</summary><p>Words that naturally occur together.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>register</summary><p>The level of formality appropriate to a context.</p></details></section><p>This is the vocabulary half of the conventions questions: choosing between words that sound alike or are frequently mixed up. There is no reasoning trick, only a list worth knowing.</p><p>The SAT also tests <strong>idiom</strong> &mdash; which preposition a word takes. <em>Different from</em>, <em>capable of</em>, <em>consists of</em>, <em>preferable to</em>, <em>concerned with</em>. Idiom errors are invisible unless you know the pairing.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>Affect</em> is usually the verb; <em>effect</em> usually the noun.</li><li><em>Their / there / they're</em> and <em>your / you're</em> &mdash; possessive, place, contraction.</li><li><em>Fewer</em> for countable, <em>less</em> for uncountable.</li><li><em>Then</em> is time; <em>than</em> is comparison.</li><li><em>Principal</em> is chief or a person; <em>principle</em> is a rule.</li><li>Learn idiomatic prepositions as fixed pairs, not by logic.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;The new policy will ___ yields, and its ___ will be measured next season.&rdquo;</em> The first is a verb (<strong>affect</strong>), the second a noun (<strong>effect</strong>). Sound gives no help here; only the grammatical role does.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Word-choice questions appear in every module, cost nothing in time, and are pure knowledge &mdash; which makes them the most efficient thing to revise the week before the test.</p></div>",
  "Common Grammar Errors": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Common Grammar Errors</strong><br><span>A short checklist catches most of what the SAT actually tests.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-conventions-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT writing guide for Common Grammar Errors\"><figcaption>Common Grammar Errors: identify, apply and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Find</strong><span>Identify the subject, verb, clauses, and modifiers.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Name</strong><span>State the exact rule being violated.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Fix</strong><span>Correct only the error without changing intended meaning.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>grammar error</summary><p>A form that violates standard sentence conventions.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>sentence fragment</summary><p>An incomplete clause presented as a sentence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>run-on sentence</summary><p>Independent clauses joined without correct punctuation.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>error pattern</summary><p>A type of mistake that occurs repeatedly.</p></details></section><p>Standard English Conventions is drawn from a small, predictable set. Knowing the list means you can scan an underlined portion and ask the right question immediately rather than reading for what &ldquo;sounds wrong&rdquo;.</p><p>Sounding wrong is unreliable: many correct SAT sentences sound stiff, and several classic errors &mdash; comma splices, ambiguous pronouns, dangling modifiers &mdash; sound perfectly natural when read aloud.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Comma splice &mdash; two independent clauses joined by a comma alone.</li><li>Subject&ndash;verb disagreement hidden behind an intervening phrase.</li><li>Ambiguous or number-mismatched pronoun.</li><li>Dangling or misplaced modifier after an opening phrase.</li><li>Faulty parallelism in a list or comparison.</li><li>Redundancy and wordiness where a shorter version exists.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;The report was thorough, it covered every district.&rdquo;</em> This reads naturally and is a comma splice. Correct it with a semicolon, a period, or <em>&ldquo;thorough<strong>, and</strong> it covered&rdquo;</em>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Roughly a quarter of Reading &amp; Writing is conventions, and this list covers most of it. Learning it converts a section that feels like intuition into one that follows rules.</p></div>",
  "Numbers and Operations": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Numbers and Operations</strong><br><span>Order of operations and sign rules underlie every Math question you will meet.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math7-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Numbers and Operations\"><figcaption>Numbers and Operations: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Order</strong><span>Parentheses → exponents → multiply/divide → add/subtract</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Property</strong><span>a(b + c) = ab + ac</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Check</strong><span>Estimate size and sign before accepting an answer.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Numbers and Operations</summary><p>Rules and properties for calculating with real numbers.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>Arithmetic on the SAT is never the point, but arithmetic mistakes cost real points. Two rules do most of the work: <strong>order of operations</strong> and the <strong>sign rules</strong> for negatives.</p><p>Order: brackets, exponents, multiplication and division left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right. Signs: two negatives multiplied or divided give a positive; subtracting a negative adds.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Order of operations: brackets &rarr; exponents &rarr; &times;&nbsp;&divide; &rarr; +&nbsp;&minus;, left to right.</li><li>Multiplication and division rank equally &mdash; work left to right, not multiplication first.</li><li>Negative &times; negative = positive; negative &times; positive = negative.</li><li>Subtracting a negative is adding: <em>5 &minus; (&minus;3) = 8</em>.</li><li>An even power of a negative is positive; an odd power stays negative.</li><li>Integers are whole numbers including negatives; zero is even and neither positive nor negative.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>8 &minus; 2 &times; 3<sup>2</sup></em>. Exponent first: 3<sup>2</sup> = 9. Then multiply: 2 &times; 9 = 18. Then subtract: 8 &minus; 18 = <strong>&minus;10</strong>. Working left to right instead gives 54, which is the trap answer.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Every algebra, geometry and data question rests on this arithmetic. A sign slip in the last line loses the mark just as completely as not knowing the method.</p></div>",
  "Fractions and Decimals": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Fractions and Decimals</strong><br><span>A fraction is a division waiting to happen; convert whenever it makes the work easier.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math7-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Fractions and Decimals\"><figcaption>Fractions and Decimals: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Convert</strong><span>3/8 = 0.375</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Add</strong><span>a/b + c/d = (ad + bc)/bd</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Check</strong><span>Use a common denominator and simplify.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Fractions and Decimals</summary><p>Equivalent ways to represent parts of a whole or non-integer values.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>Adding or subtracting fractions needs a common denominator; multiplying does not. To divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal.</p><p>Decimals and fractions are interchangeable, and the SAT often makes one form far easier than the other. Knowing the common conversions by heart saves calculator time.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Add and subtract only over a <strong>common denominator</strong>.</li><li>Multiply fractions straight across; divide by multiplying by the reciprocal.</li><li>Learn by heart: 1/2 = 0.5, 1/4 = 0.25, 3/4 = 0.75, 1/5 = 0.2, 1/8 = 0.125.</li><li>A fraction inside a fraction is just a division &mdash; rewrite it.</li><li>Simplify before multiplying; cancelling first keeps the numbers small.</li><li>Student-produced responses accept a fraction or a decimal, so give whichever is exact.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>2/3 &divide; 4/9</em> = <em>2/3 &times; 9/4</em>. Cancel 3 into 9 and 2 into 4: <em>1/1 &times; 3/2</em> = <strong>3/2</strong>. Cancelling first avoids multiplying 18/12 and simplifying afterwards.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Fraction work appears throughout Math, and in student-produced responses an unsimplified or rounded answer can be marked wrong where the exact fraction would score.</p></div>",
  "Percentages": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Percentages</strong><br><span>Percent means per hundred, and every percent question is one of three shapes.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math7-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Percentages\"><figcaption>Percentages: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Meaning</strong><span>p% = p/100</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Change</strong><span>percent change = change/original × 100%</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Example</strong><span>80 increased by 25% = 100</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Percentages</summary><p>Ratios expressed per one hundred.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>The three shapes are: find the percent of a number, find what percent one number is of another, and find the original after a percent change. All three come from <em>part = percent &times; whole</em>.</p><p>Percent <strong>increase</strong> multiplies by (1 + rate); percent <strong>decrease</strong> multiplies by (1 &minus; rate). Successive changes multiply &mdash; they never add.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>part = percent &times; whole</em>; rearrange for whichever is missing.</li><li>Increase by 20% &rarr; &times; 1.20. Decrease by 20% &rarr; &times; 0.80.</li><li>Successive percent changes <strong>multiply</strong>: +20% then &minus;20% is &times;1.2&times;0.8 = 0.96.</li><li>Percent change = (new &minus; old) &divide; old &times; 100.</li><li>To reverse a percent change, divide rather than applying the opposite percent.</li><li>&ldquo;Of&rdquo; means multiply; &ldquo;is&rdquo; means equals.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A price rises 20% then falls 20%. Not back to the start: 1.20 &times; 0.80 = <strong>0.96</strong>, so it ends 4% below the original. This is the most commonly tested percent trap on the SAT.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Percent problems appear in Problem Solving and Data Analysis, which is a quarter of the Math section, and the multiply-not-add rule settles most of the hard ones.</p></div>",
  "M7:Ratios": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 M7:Ratios</strong><br><span>A ratio compares parts; a fraction compares a part with the whole.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math7-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Ratios\"><figcaption>Ratios: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Ratio</strong><span>a:b = a/b</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Scale</strong><span>Equivalent ratios multiply both terms equally.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Example</strong><span>3:5 = 12:20</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Ratios</summary><p>Comparisons of two quantities by division.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>A ratio <em>a : b</em> says how quantities compare, not how large they are. To turn a ratio into actual amounts, add the parts to find the total number of shares, then divide.</p><p>The commonest error is treating a ratio as a fraction of the whole. In a ratio of 3 : 5 there are eight shares, so the first quantity is 3/8 of the total, not 3/5.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Add the ratio parts to get the number of shares.</li><li>Part of the total = its share &divide; total shares.</li><li>Ratios simplify like fractions: 8 : 12 = 2 : 3.</li><li>Keep the order: 3 : 5 is not the same as 5 : 3.</li><li>To scale a ratio, multiply every part by the same number.</li><li>A ratio has no units of its own; the quantities it compares do.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Rice and beans are planted in a ratio of 3 : 5 across 64 hectares. Shares: 3 + 5 = 8. One share = 64 &divide; 8 = 8 hectares. Rice = 3 &times; 8 = <strong>24 hectares</strong>, not 3/5 of 64.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Ratios underpin proportions, rates and scale problems, all of which appear in Problem Solving and Data Analysis.</p></div>",
  "Exponents": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Exponents</strong><br><span>Five rules cover almost every exponent question on the test.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math7-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Exponents\"><figcaption>Exponents: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Product</strong><span>aᵐ · aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Power</strong><span>(aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Negative</strong><span>a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Exponents</summary><p>Notation showing repeated multiplication by a base.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>Exponents count repeated multiplication. The SAT tests the rules for combining them far more often than it tests computing large powers.</p><p>Negative exponents mean reciprocals, and fractional exponents mean roots. Both look harder than they are once translated: <em>x<sup>&minus;2</sup> = 1/x<sup>2</sup></em> and <em>x<sup>1/2</sup> = &radic;x</em>.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Multiply same base: <strong>add</strong> exponents &mdash; <em>x<sup>a</sup>&middot;x<sup>b</sup> = x<sup>a+b</sup></em>.</li><li>Divide same base: <strong>subtract</strong> &mdash; <em>x<sup>a</sup>/x<sup>b</sup> = x<sup>a&minus;b</sup></em>.</li><li>Power of a power: <strong>multiply</strong> &mdash; <em>(x<sup>a</sup>)<sup>b</sup> = x<sup>ab</sup></em>.</li><li><em>x<sup>0</sup> = 1</em> for any non-zero x.</li><li><em>x<sup>&minus;n</sup> = 1/x<sup>n</sup></em>; <em>x<sup>1/n</sup> = <sup>n</sup>&radic;x</em>.</li><li>Rules apply only to the <strong>same base</strong> &mdash; rewrite 4 as 2<sup>2</sup> when needed.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>(2<sup>3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> &divide; 2<sup>4</sup></em>. Power of a power: 2<sup>6</sup>. Divide: 2<sup>6&minus;4</sup> = 2<sup>2</sup> = <strong>4</strong>. Adding the exponents instead of multiplying would give 2<sup>5</sup>, the standard wrong answer.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Exponent rules recur throughout Algebra and Advanced Math, especially in exponential functions and radical expressions.</p></div>",
  "M7:Radicals": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 M7:Radicals</strong><br><span>A radical is a fractional exponent, which is why the exponent rules still work.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math7-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Radicals\"><figcaption>Radicals: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Root</strong><span>√a · √b = √(ab)</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Simplify</strong><span>√72 = √(36·2) = 6√2</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Check</strong><span>(√a)² = a for a ≥ 0</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Radicals</summary><p>Expressions containing roots, such as square roots.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>&radic;x means the non-negative number whose square is x. Radicals simplify by pulling out perfect-square factors: &radic;48 = &radic;(16&middot;3) = 4&radic;3.</p><p>Because &radic;x = x<sup>1/2</sup>, every exponent rule applies. Radicals multiply and divide freely; they do <strong>not</strong> add unless the radicands match.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Simplify by extracting perfect-square factors.</li><li>&radic;a &middot; &radic;b = &radic;(ab); &radic;a &divide; &radic;b = &radic;(a/b).</li><li>&radic;a + &radic;b is <strong>not</strong> &radic;(a+b).</li><li>Like radicals add: 2&radic;3 + 4&radic;3 = 6&radic;3.</li><li>&radic;x = x<sup>1/2</sup>, so exponent rules apply.</li><li>Rationalise a denominator by multiplying top and bottom by the radical.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">&radic;48 + &radic;27 = 4&radic;3 + 3&radic;3 = <strong>7&radic;3</strong>. Adding under one root to get &radic;75 is wrong &mdash; though notice &radic;75 = 5&radic;3, which is a different number.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Radicals appear in Advanced Math and in geometry through the Pythagorean theorem, where exact radical answers are frequently required.</p></div>",
  "Scientific Notation": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Scientific Notation</strong><br><span>A compact way to write very large and very small numbers, and the SAT expects the standard form.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math7-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Scientific Notation\"><figcaption>Scientific Notation: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Form</strong><span>a × 10ⁿ where 1 ≤ |a| < 10</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Large</strong><span>4,500,000 = 4.5 × 10⁶</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Small</strong><span>0.0032 = 3.2 × 10⁻³</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Scientific Notation</summary><p>A compact form using a coefficient multiplied by a power of ten.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>Scientific notation writes a number as <em>a &times; 10<sup>n</sup></em> where 1 &le; a &lt; 10. A positive exponent means a large number, a negative exponent a small one.</p><p>To multiply, multiply the coefficients and add the exponents. To divide, divide the coefficients and subtract. Then renormalise so the coefficient sits between 1 and 10.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Standard form requires <strong>1 &le; a &lt; 10</strong>.</li><li>Positive exponent &rarr; large number; negative exponent &rarr; small number.</li><li>Multiplying: multiply coefficients, <strong>add</strong> exponents.</li><li>Dividing: divide coefficients, <strong>subtract</strong> exponents.</li><li>Renormalise afterwards: 12 &times; 10<sup>4</sup> becomes 1.2 &times; 10<sup>5</sup>.</li><li>Moving the decimal right decreases the exponent; moving it left increases it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">(3 &times; 10<sup>4</sup>)(4 &times; 10<sup>3</sup>) = 12 &times; 10<sup>7</sup>, which is not standard form. Renormalise: <strong>1.2 &times; 10<sup>8</sup></strong>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Scientific notation appears in science-context Math questions and in data interpretation, where large populations and small measurements are routine.</p></div>",
  "Calculator Skills": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Calculator Skills</strong><br><span>Desmos is available for the whole Math section &mdash; knowing when not to use it matters as much as knowing how.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math7-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Calculator Skills\"><figcaption>Calculator Skills: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Model</strong><span>Enter the expression exactly as written.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Graph</strong><span>Use Desmos to locate intercepts and intersections.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Verify</strong><span>Confirm the result satisfies the original problem.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Calculator Skills</summary><p>Accurate and strategic use of the built-in Desmos calculator.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>The Bluebook app provides the Desmos graphing calculator throughout Math. It graphs equations, finds intersections and zeros, evaluates expressions and handles regressions.</p><p>It is fastest on problems that are slow by hand: solving quadratics, finding where two lines meet, reading a maximum. It is slower than your head on arithmetic, so use it selectively.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Type an equation and Desmos graphs it; click a point to read its coordinates.</li><li>Solve equations by graphing both sides and finding the intersection.</li><li>Find roots by graphing <em>y = expression</em> and reading the x-intercepts.</li><li>Use sliders for a parameter when a question asks how a graph changes.</li><li>Do not reach for it on simple arithmetic &mdash; typing costs more time than thinking.</li><li>Practise inside Bluebook so the interface is familiar on test day.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">For <em>2x + 5 = 3x &minus; 4</em>, graph <em>y = 2x + 5</em> and <em>y = 3x &minus; 4</em> and read the intersection at x = <strong>9</strong>. Faster and safer than rearranging under time pressure.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Candidates who practised in Desmos convert several hard questions per section into fast ones. Those meeting it for the first time on test day lose time learning it.</p></div>",
  "Formula Review": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Formula Review</strong><br><span>Bluebook gives you a reference sheet &mdash; know what is on it and what is not.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math7-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Formula Review\"><figcaption>Formula Review: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Identify</strong><span>Name the quantities and units.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Select</strong><span>Choose a formula connecting knowns and unknown.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Substitute</strong><span>Insert values, solve, then check units.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Formula Review</summary><p>Selection and use of equations representing mathematical relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>The Digital SAT provides a built-in reference sheet covering common geometry formulas: areas, circumference, volumes, the Pythagorean theorem and special right triangles.</p><p>It does <strong>not</strong> provide the algebra you need. Slope, the quadratic formula, percent change, averages, distance and probability all have to come from memory.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Provided</strong>: circle area and circumference, triangle and rectangle area, volumes, Pythagoras, special right triangles.</li><li><strong>Not provided</strong>: slope = (y&#8322;&minus;y&#8321;)/(x&#8322;&minus;x&#8321;).</li><li><strong>Not provided</strong>: quadratic formula <em>x = (&minus;b &plusmn; &radic;(b&sup2;&minus;4ac)) / 2a</em>.</li><li><strong>Not provided</strong>: percent change, mean, distance = rate &times; time.</li><li>Slope-intercept form <em>y = mx + b</em> and vertex form <em>y = a(x&minus;h)&sup2; + k</em>.</li><li>Learn the short unprovided list rather than the long provided one.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A student memorises circle and volume formulas &mdash; all on the reference sheet &mdash; but not the slope formula, which is not. The revision effort went entirely into what the app was going to hand him anyway.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Knowing which formulas the app supplies tells you exactly what to memorise, and it is a much shorter list than most candidates assume.</p></div>",
  "Linear Equations": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Linear Equations</strong><br><span>Isolate the variable by undoing operations in reverse order.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math8-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Linear Equations\"><figcaption>Linear Equations: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Form</strong><span>ax + b = c</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Solve</strong><span>ax = c − b → x = (c − b)/a</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Check</strong><span>Substitute x into the original equation.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Linear Equations</summary><p>Equations whose variables have first power and graph as straight lines.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>A linear equation has the variable to the first power only. Solve by doing the same thing to both sides until the variable stands alone, undoing addition before multiplication.</p><p>Linear equations are the single most common Math topic on the SAT, appearing directly and inside word problems, systems and graph questions.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Undo addition and subtraction first, then multiplication and division.</li><li>Whatever you do to one side, do to the other.</li><li>Distribute before collecting like terms.</li><li>Clear fractions by multiplying every term by the common denominator.</li><li>An equation reducing to <em>0 = 0</em> has infinitely many solutions; to <em>0 = 5</em>, none.</li><li>Always substitute your answer back to check.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>3(x &minus; 2) + 4 = 19</em>. Distribute: 3x &minus; 6 + 4 = 19. Collect: 3x &minus; 2 = 19. Add 2: 3x = 21. Divide: x = <strong>7</strong>. Substituting back gives 3(5) + 4 = 19. ✓</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Linear equations are the foundation of the whole Algebra domain, which is the largest part of the Math section.</p></div>",
  "Linear Inequalities": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Linear Inequalities</strong><br><span>Solve exactly like an equation, with one rule that catches everyone.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math8-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Linear Inequalities\"><figcaption>Linear Inequalities: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Solve</strong><span>Treat like an equation.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reverse</strong><span>Flip the sign after multiplying/dividing by a negative.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Graph</strong><span>Use open or closed endpoints correctly.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Linear Inequalities</summary><p>Comparisons producing a range of values rather than one equality.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>Inequalities follow the same steps as equations, except that multiplying or dividing both sides by a <strong>negative</strong> number reverses the inequality sign.</p><p>Solutions are ranges rather than single values. On a number line, &lt; and &gt; take an open circle; &le; and &ge; take a closed one.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Multiplying or dividing by a negative <strong>flips</strong> the sign.</li><li>Adding or subtracting never flips it.</li><li>Solutions are intervals, not single values.</li><li>Open circle for strict inequalities, closed for &le; and &ge;.</li><li>&ldquo;At least&rdquo; means &ge;; &ldquo;at most&rdquo; means &le;; &ldquo;more than&rdquo; means &gt;.</li><li>Check by substituting any value from your range.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&minus;2x + 3 &gt; 11</em>. Subtract 3: &minus;2x &gt; 8. Divide by &minus;2 and <strong>flip</strong>: x &lt; &minus;4. Forgetting the flip gives x &gt; &minus;4, which is the trap answer offered every time.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Inequalities appear alone and inside word problems about budgets, capacities and minimum requirements, where the phrase-to-symbol translation matters as much as the algebra.</p></div>",
  "Systems of Equations": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Systems of Equations</strong><br><span>Two equations, two unknowns &mdash; substitute or eliminate, whichever is tidier.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math8-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Systems of Equations\"><figcaption>Systems of Equations: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Substitution</strong><span>Solve one equation for a variable.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Elimination</strong><span>Add equations to remove a variable.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Meaning</strong><span>Intersection = shared solution.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Systems of Equations</summary><p>Two or more equations solved together for shared values.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>A system asks for the values satisfying both equations at once, which graphically is where the two lines meet. Solve by <strong>substitution</strong> (isolate one variable and substitute) or <strong>elimination</strong> (add or subtract to cancel a variable).</p><p>Choose by shape: substitution when a variable is already isolated or has coefficient 1; elimination when coefficients line up or can be matched easily.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Substitution: isolate one variable, put it into the other equation.</li><li>Elimination: add or subtract the equations to cancel one variable.</li><li>The solution is the point where the lines intersect.</li><li>Parallel lines (same slope, different intercept) &rarr; <strong>no solution</strong>.</li><li>Identical lines &rarr; infinitely many solutions.</li><li>Desmos solves these instantly by graphing both equations.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>x + y = 10</em> and <em>2x &minus; y = 5</em>. Adding eliminates y: 3x = 15, so x = 5, and then y = <strong>5</strong>. The pair (5, 5) satisfies both.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Systems are a guaranteed presence in the Algebra domain, and the no-solution / infinite-solution cases are tested as concept questions rather than computations.</p></div>",
  "Functions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Functions</strong><br><span>A function is a rule: one input, exactly one output.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math8-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Functions\"><figcaption>Functions: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Notation</strong><span>f(x) gives the output for input x.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evaluate</strong><span>If f(x)=2x+3, f(4)=11.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Domain</strong><span>List all allowed input values.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Functions</summary><p>Rules assigning exactly one output to each allowed input.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p><em>f(x)</em> is notation, not multiplication. <em>f(3)</em> means substitute 3 wherever x appears. The <strong>domain</strong> is the allowed inputs; the <strong>range</strong> is the outputs produced.</p><p>Composite functions apply one rule then another: <em>f(g(2))</em> means evaluate g at 2 first, then apply f to that result. Work from the inside out.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>f(x)</em> means the output of the rule f for input x &mdash; never f times x.</li><li>To evaluate, substitute the value everywhere x appears.</li><li>Composite: work inside out &mdash; <em>f(g(2))</em> needs g(2) first.</li><li>Domain excludes anything making a denominator zero or a square root negative.</li><li>The <strong>vertical line test</strong>: a graph is a function if no vertical line meets it twice.</li><li>Finding x when f(x) = k means solving the equation, not evaluating it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">If <em>f(x) = 2x + 1</em> and <em>g(x) = x&sup2;</em>, then <em>f(g(3))</em> = f(9) = 2(9) + 1 = <strong>19</strong>. Reversing the order gives g(f(3)) = g(7) = 49 &mdash; a different question with a different answer.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Function notation runs through Algebra and Advanced Math, and the inside-out rule for composites is tested every time it appears.</p></div>",
  "Graphs": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Graphs</strong><br><span>Slope and intercept tell you almost everything a linear graph can say.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math8-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Graphs\"><figcaption>Graphs: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Slope</strong><span>m = (y₂−y₁)/(x₂−x₁)</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Intercept</strong><span>Where a graph crosses an axis.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Read</strong><span>Match axes, scale, and units before calculating.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Graphs</summary><p>Visual representations of relationships between variables.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>In <em>y = mx + b</em>, <strong>m</strong> is the slope and <strong>b</strong> the y-intercept. Slope is rise over run: (y&#8322;&minus;y&#8321;)/(x&#8322;&minus;x&#8321;). This formula is <em>not</em> on the reference sheet.</p><p>In context questions, slope is a rate of change and the intercept is a starting value. Reading a word problem into those two roles solves most linear-modelling questions immediately.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>y = mx + b</em>: m is slope, b is where the line crosses the y-axis.</li><li>Slope = (y&#8322;&minus;y&#8321;)/(x&#8322;&minus;x&#8321;) &mdash; memorise it, it is not provided.</li><li>Positive slope rises left to right; negative falls; zero is horizontal.</li><li>A vertical line has undefined slope.</li><li>Parallel lines share a slope; perpendicular slopes multiply to &minus;1.</li><li>In context: slope = rate of change, intercept = starting value.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A phone plan costs $15 plus $0.10 per minute: <em>y = 0.10x + 15</em>. The intercept 15 is the fixed monthly charge and the slope 0.10 is the cost per minute &mdash; the two roles the SAT asks you to identify.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Linear graphs and their interpretation appear throughout Algebra and again in Problem Solving and Data Analysis as modelling questions.</p></div>",
  "Quadratic Equations": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Quadratic Equations</strong><br><span>Three methods; pick the one the numbers invite.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math8-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Quadratic Equations\"><figcaption>Quadratic Equations: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Standard</strong><span>ax² + bx + c = 0</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Factor</strong><span>(x−r₁)(x−r₂)=0</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Formula</strong><span>x = (−b ± √(b²−4ac))/2a</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Quadratic Equations</summary><p>Second-degree equations containing a nonzero squared-variable term.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>A quadratic has the form <em>ax&sup2; + bx + c = 0</em>. Solve by <strong>factoring</strong> when the numbers are friendly, by the <strong>quadratic formula</strong> when they are not, or by <strong>graphing</strong> in Desmos to read the x-intercepts.</p><p>The quadratic formula is <em>x = (&minus;b &plusmn; &radic;(b&sup2;&minus;4ac)) / 2a</em> and is not on the reference sheet. The discriminant <em>b&sup2;&minus;4ac</em> alone tells you how many real solutions exist.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Factor first if the numbers are small &mdash; it is fastest.</li><li>Quadratic formula always works: memorise it, it is not provided.</li><li>Discriminant &gt; 0 &rarr; two real solutions; = 0 &rarr; one; &lt; 0 &rarr; none.</li><li>The solutions are the x-intercepts of the parabola.</li><li>Set the equation to zero before doing anything else.</li><li>Desmos reads the roots straight off a graph.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>x&sup2; &minus; 5x + 6 = 0</em> factors to (x&minus;2)(x&minus;3) = 0, giving x = 2 or x = <strong>3</strong>. The discriminant is 25 &minus; 24 = 1, positive, confirming two real solutions before any work.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Quadratics are the core of Advanced Math and appear in Algebra as well, in equations, functions and modelling questions alike.</p></div>",
  "Polynomial Expressions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Polynomial Expressions</strong><br><span>Combine like terms, distribute carefully, and watch the signs.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math8-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Polynomial Expressions\"><figcaption>Polynomial Expressions: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Combine</strong><span>Add only like terms.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Multiply</strong><span>Use distribution for every term.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Factor</strong><span>Reverse multiplication to reveal structure.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Polynomial Expressions</summary><p>Sums of terms with variables raised to whole-number powers.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>A polynomial is a sum of terms of the form <em>ax<sup>n</sup></em>. Adding and subtracting means combining <strong>like terms</strong> &mdash; those with the same variable and exponent. Multiplying means distributing every term across every other.</p><p>The commonest error is a sign: subtracting a polynomial requires distributing the minus sign across every term inside the brackets, not just the first.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Like terms share the same variable <em>and</em> exponent: 3x&sup2; and 5x&sup2;, not 3x&sup2; and 3x.</li><li>Subtracting a polynomial distributes the minus across <strong>every</strong> term.</li><li>Multiply two binomials by distributing each term (FOIL).</li><li>The degree of a polynomial is its highest exponent.</li><li>Common factoring patterns: difference of squares, common factor, trinomials.</li><li>Check by substituting a simple value such as x = 1 or x = 2 into both forms.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>(3x&sup2; + 2x) &minus; (x&sup2; &minus; 4x)</em> = 3x&sup2; + 2x &minus; x&sup2; <strong>+</strong> 4x = 2x&sup2; + 6x. Failing to flip the sign on &minus;4x gives 2x&sup2; &minus; 2x, the standard wrong answer.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Polynomial manipulation supports factoring, quadratics and rational expressions, so an error habit here spreads across the whole Advanced Math domain.</p></div>",
  "Word Problems": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Word Problems</strong><br><span>Translate the sentence into an equation before you calculate anything.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math8-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Word Problems\"><figcaption>Word Problems: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Define</strong><span>Let x represent the unknown with units.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Translate</strong><span>Turn relationships into equations.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Interpret</strong><span>Answer the real question, not only for x.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Word Problems</summary><p>Real situations translated into mathematical relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>Most Math word problems fail at translation, not arithmetic. Read once for the situation, then identify what the unknown is, name it, and build an equation phrase by phrase.</p><p>The key phrases are consistent: <em>is</em> means equals, <em>of</em> means multiply, <em>per</em> means divide or a rate, <em>more than</em> means add, <em>less than</em> reverses the order of subtraction.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Name the unknown explicitly: <em>let x = the number of hectares</em>.</li><li><em>is</em> &rarr; =, <em>of</em> &rarr; &times;, <em>per</em> &rarr; rate, <em>more than</em> &rarr; +.</li><li><em>&ldquo;5 less than x&rdquo;</em> is <em>x &minus; 5</em>, not <em>5 &minus; x</em>.</li><li>Check what the question actually asks &mdash; often not the variable you solved for.</li><li>Include units and confirm the answer is plausible in context.</li><li>Rate problems: distance = rate &times; time; work = rate &times; time.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&ldquo;Seven less than three times a number is 14.&rdquo;</em> Let the number be x: <em>3x &minus; 7 = 14</em>, so 3x = 21 and x = <strong>7</strong>. Writing <em>7 &minus; 3x</em> instead is the classic translation error.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Word problems are distributed across every Math domain. Translating carefully is worth more than any calculation shortcut, because a correctly built equation is nearly always easy to solve.</p></div>",
  "Quadratic Functions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Quadratic Functions</strong><br><span>The same equation in three forms, each telling you something different.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math9-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Quadratic Functions\"><figcaption>Quadratic Functions: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Form</strong><span>f(x)=ax²+bx+c</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Vertex</strong><span>x = −b/(2a)</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Shape</strong><span>a>0 opens up; a<0 opens down.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Quadratic Functions</summary><p>Functions whose graphs are parabolas.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p><strong>Standard form</strong> <em>y = ax&sup2; + bx + c</em> shows the y-intercept c. <strong>Factored form</strong> <em>y = a(x&minus;p)(x&minus;q)</em> shows the x-intercepts p and q. <strong>Vertex form</strong> <em>y = a(x&minus;h)&sup2; + k</em> shows the vertex (h, k).</p><p>The SAT asks which form reveals a given feature most readily. The axis of symmetry is <em>x = &minus;b/2a</em>, and the vertex lies on it. If a &gt; 0 the parabola opens upward and the vertex is a minimum; if a &lt; 0 it opens downward and the vertex is a maximum.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Standard form reveals the <strong>y-intercept</strong>.</li><li>Factored form reveals the <strong>x-intercepts</strong>.</li><li>Vertex form reveals the <strong>vertex</strong>, the maximum or minimum.</li><li>Axis of symmetry: <em>x = &minus;b/2a</em>.</li><li>a &gt; 0 opens upward (minimum); a &lt; 0 opens downward (maximum).</li><li>The vertex x-value gives the input at which the output is largest or smallest.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>y = &minus;2(x &minus; 3)&sup2; + 8</em> is in vertex form: the vertex is (3, 8) and, because a is negative, that 8 is a <strong>maximum</strong>. No calculation needed &mdash; the form states it.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Quadratic functions are central to Advanced Math, and 'which form shows this feature' is a question type in its own right.</p></div>",
  "Exponential Functions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Exponential Functions</strong><br><span>Repeated multiplication, not repeated addition &mdash; that is the whole distinction.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math9-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Exponential Functions\"><figcaption>Exponential Functions: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Form</strong><span>f(x)=a·bˣ</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Growth</strong><span>b>1</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Decay</strong><span>0<b<1</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Exponential Functions</summary><p>Functions in which the variable appears in the exponent.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>An exponential function has the form <em>y = a&middot;b<sup>x</sup></em>, where a is the starting value and b the growth factor. If b &gt; 1 the quantity grows; if 0 &lt; b &lt; 1 it decays.</p><p>The SAT tests the contrast with linear models constantly. A linear model adds the same amount each period; an exponential model multiplies by the same factor each period.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>y = a&middot;b<sup>x</sup></em>: a is the initial value, b the factor per period.</li><li>b &gt; 1 is growth; 0 &lt; b &lt; 1 is decay.</li><li>Growth of 5% per year &rarr; b = 1.05. Decay of 5% &rarr; b = 0.95.</li><li>Linear adds a constant; exponential multiplies by a constant.</li><li>At x = 0 the value is a, the y-intercept.</li><li>&ldquo;Doubles each period&rdquo; means b = 2; &ldquo;halves&rdquo; means b = 0.5.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A population of 500 grows 8% per year: <em>P = 500(1.08)<sup>t</sup></em>. After 3 years, 500(1.08)&sup3; &asymp; <strong>630</strong>. A linear model adding 8% of the original each year would give 620 &mdash; close, and wrong.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Exponential models appear in Advanced Math and in the data-analysis questions about growth, decay and compound interest.</p></div>",
  "Nonlinear Equations": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Nonlinear Equations</strong><br><span>When the graph is not a line, the number of solutions is part of the question.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math9-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Nonlinear Equations\"><figcaption>Nonlinear Equations: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Recognize</strong><span>Variable powers, roots, or products create nonlinearity.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Solve</strong><span>Factor, isolate, graph, or substitute.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Check</strong><span>Test every candidate; reject extraneous solutions.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Nonlinear Equations</summary><p>Equations whose graphs are not straight lines.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>Nonlinear equations include quadratics, higher-degree polynomials, radicals and rational equations. Their graphs curve, so a system pairing a line with a curve may have two solutions, one, or none.</p><p>Radical and rational equations require a check: squaring both sides or clearing a denominator can introduce <strong>extraneous solutions</strong> that satisfy the new equation but not the original.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>A line and a parabola may intersect twice, once, or not at all.</li><li>Always substitute solutions back into the <em>original</em> equation.</li><li>Squaring both sides can create extraneous solutions.</li><li>Clearing a denominator can create a solution that makes it zero &mdash; reject it.</li><li>Desmos shows the number of intersections immediately.</li><li>Set one side to zero and find x-intercepts when solving graphically.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&radic;(x + 6) = x</em>. Squaring gives x&sup2; &minus; x &minus; 6 = 0, so x = 3 or x = &minus;2. Checking, &radic;9 = 3 ✓ but &radic;4 = 2 &ne; &minus;2 ✗. Only x = <strong>3</strong> is a solution.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The extraneous-solution check is tested deliberately: the SAT offers the rejected value as an answer choice every time.</p></div>",
  "Rational Expressions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Rational Expressions</strong><br><span>A fraction with variables &mdash; and a denominator that must never be zero.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math9-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Rational Expressions\"><figcaption>Rational Expressions: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Restrict</strong><span>Denominator cannot equal zero.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Simplify</strong><span>Factor before canceling common factors.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Operate</strong><span>Use a common denominator for addition.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Rational Expressions</summary><p>Fractions whose numerator or denominator contains polynomials.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>Rational expressions are ratios of polynomials. Simplify by factoring the top and bottom and cancelling common factors. Only <strong>factors</strong> cancel, never individual terms.</p><p>Any value making the original denominator zero is excluded from the domain, and remains excluded even after cancelling.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Factor fully before cancelling anything.</li><li>Cancel <strong>factors</strong>, never terms across a plus or minus sign.</li><li>Excluded values come from the <em>original</em> denominator.</li><li>Add or subtract only over a common denominator.</li><li>Divide by multiplying by the reciprocal.</li><li>The expression is undefined wherever the denominator equals zero.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>(x&sup2; &minus; 9)/(x &minus; 3)</em> = <em>(x&minus;3)(x+3)/(x&minus;3)</em> = <strong>x + 3</strong>, but x = 3 is still excluded because the original denominator was zero there.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Rational expressions appear in Advanced Math, and the excluded-value point is tested as a concept question far more often than the algebra itself.</p></div>",
  "M9:Radicals": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 M9:Radicals</strong><br><span>Radical equations at Advanced Math level: isolate, square, and always check.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math9-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Radicals\"><figcaption>Radicals: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Isolate</strong><span>Put the radical alone on one side.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Square</strong><span>Raise both sides carefully.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Verify</strong><span>Check for extraneous solutions.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Radicals</summary><p>Root expressions used in advanced equations and transformations.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>Module 7 covered simplifying radicals. Here they appear inside equations, where the method is to <strong>isolate the radical first</strong>, then square both sides, then solve what remains.</p><p>Squaring is the dangerous step. It can turn a false statement into a true one, so every solution must be substituted back into the original equation. The SAT reliably offers the extraneous root as a choice.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Isolate the radical before squaring &mdash; never square a sum term by term.</li><li>(&radic;a)&sup2; = a, but (a + &radic;b)&sup2; requires full expansion.</li><li>Squaring may create <strong>extraneous</strong> solutions; check every answer.</li><li>Two radicals may need squaring twice, isolating one each time.</li><li>A square root cannot equal a negative number.</li><li>Fractional exponents are radicals: <em>x<sup>2/3</sup> = <sup>3</sup>&radic;(x&sup2;)</em>.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>&radic;(2x + 3) &minus; 1 = 4</em>. Isolate: &radic;(2x + 3) = 5. Square: 2x + 3 = 25. Solve: x = <strong>11</strong>. Check: &radic;25 &minus; 1 = 4 ✓. Squaring before isolating would have produced a much messier and wrong equation.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Radical equations combine the exponent rules, the extraneous-root check and careful algebra, which is why they appear at the harder end of the Math section.</p></div>",
  "Factoring": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Factoring</strong><br><span>Recognise the pattern and the factorisation writes itself.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math9-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Factoring\"><figcaption>Factoring: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>GCF</strong><span>Remove the greatest common factor first.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Trinomial</strong><span>x²+bx+c = (x+m)(x+n)</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Difference</strong><span>a²−b²=(a−b)(a+b)</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Factoring</summary><p>Rewriting an expression as a product of simpler factors.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>Factoring reverses multiplication. Always look for a <strong>common factor</strong> first, then check for a recognisable pattern before attempting trial and error.</p><p>The three patterns worth knowing cold are the difference of squares, the perfect-square trinomial, and the simple trinomial where you seek two numbers multiplying to c and adding to b.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Common factor first: <em>2x&sup2; + 6x = 2x(x + 3)</em>.</li><li>Difference of squares: <em>a&sup2; &minus; b&sup2; = (a&minus;b)(a+b)</em>.</li><li>Perfect square: <em>a&sup2; &plusmn; 2ab + b&sup2; = (a &plusmn; b)&sup2;</em>.</li><li>Simple trinomial <em>x&sup2; + bx + c</em>: find two numbers multiplying to c, adding to b.</li><li>A sum of squares <em>a&sup2; + b&sup2;</em> does <strong>not</strong> factor over the reals.</li><li>Check by expanding your factors back out.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>x&sup2; &minus; 16</em> is a difference of squares: <strong>(x &minus; 4)(x + 4)</strong>. But <em>x&sup2; + 16</em> does not factor at all over the real numbers &mdash; a distinction the SAT tests directly.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Factoring underlies quadratics, rational expressions and polynomial questions, so pattern recognition here speeds up the whole Advanced Math domain.</p></div>",
  "Advanced Word Problems": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Advanced Word Problems</strong><br><span>Harder wrapping, same method: name the unknown and build the equation.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math9-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT mathematics guide for Advanced Word Problems\"><figcaption>Advanced Word Problems: formula, method and check</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Represent</strong><span>Define variables and constraints.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Model</strong><span>Build equations or functions from relationships.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evaluate</strong><span>Check units, reasonableness, and required form.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Advanced Word Problems</summary><p>Multi-step situations requiring algebraic modeling and interpretation.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A symbol representing an unknown or changing quantity.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>equation</summary><p>A statement that two mathematical expressions have equal value.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>verification</summary><p>Checking a result in the original conditions to confirm it is valid.</p></details></section><p>Advanced word problems involve quadratic, exponential or systems models rather than single linear equations. The translation discipline is identical; only the model changes.</p><p>Read for the <strong>shape</strong> of the relationship. Constant addition per period means linear. Constant multiplication means exponential. A product of two varying quantities, or an area, usually means quadratic.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Constant amount per period &rarr; linear model.</li><li>Constant percentage or factor per period &rarr; exponential model.</li><li>Area, product of two changing quantities, or projectile height &rarr; quadratic.</li><li>Two unknowns with two conditions &rarr; a system.</li><li>Define every variable in words before writing any equation.</li><li>Reject solutions that are impossible in context, such as a negative length.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A rectangular plot has a perimeter of 40 m and an area of 96 m&sup2;. Let width be w: length is 20 &minus; w, so <em>w(20 &minus; w) = 96</em>, giving w&sup2; &minus; 20w + 96 = 0 and w = 8 or 12. Both are valid &mdash; the same rectangle, <strong>8 m by 12 m</strong>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>These questions sit at the top of the Math difficulty range, and the marks go to candidates who identify the model before touching the algebra.</p></div>",
  "M10:Ratios": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 M10:Ratios</strong><br><span>Ratios in context: rates, unit conversions and scaling.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math10-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Ratios\"><figcaption>Ratios: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Compare</strong><span>a:b means a/b.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Unit rate</strong><span>Divide to find the amount for one unit.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Scale</strong><span>Multiply both terms by the same factor.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Ratios</summary><p>Comparisons showing the relative size of two quantities.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dataset</summary><p>A collection of values gathered for analysis.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A characteristic or quantity that can change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>interpretation</summary><p>A conclusion drawn carefully from mathematical evidence.</p></details></section><p>Module 7 covered the mechanics; here ratios appear as <strong>rates</strong> &mdash; kilometres per hour, dollars per kilogram, people per square kilometre &mdash; and as scale factors in maps and models.</p><p>The reliable technique is to set up the ratio with units written in, then cancel units the way you cancel numbers. If the units of your answer are wrong, the setup is wrong regardless of the arithmetic.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Write units into every ratio and cancel them as you would numbers.</li><li>A <strong>unit rate</strong> has 1 in the denominator: cost per item, distance per hour.</li><li>To convert, multiply by a fraction equal to 1: <em>1 km / 1000 m</em>.</li><li>Scale factor for length k means area scales by k&sup2; and volume by k&sup3;.</li><li>Check plausibility: an answer thousands of times too large signals an inverted ratio.</li><li>Rates can be combined by adding work per unit time, not by adding times.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A vehicle uses 6 litres per 100 km. For 250 km: <em>250 km &times; (6 L / 100 km)</em> = <strong>15 L</strong>. The km cancel, leaving litres &mdash; confirming the ratio was set up the right way up.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Rates and unit conversion appear throughout Problem Solving and Data Analysis, which is roughly a quarter of the Math section.</p></div>",
  "Proportions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Proportions</strong><br><span>Two equal ratios; cross-multiply and solve.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math10-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Proportions\"><figcaption>Proportions: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Set equal</strong><span>a/b = c/d</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Solve</strong><span>Cross-multiply: ad = bc</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Check</strong><span>Both ratios must represent matching quantities.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Proportions</summary><p>Equations stating that two ratios are equal.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dataset</summary><p>A collection of values gathered for analysis.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A characteristic or quantity that can change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>interpretation</summary><p>A conclusion drawn carefully from mathematical evidence.</p></details></section><p>A proportion states that two ratios are equal: <em>a/b = c/d</em>. Cross-multiplying gives <em>ad = bc</em>, which turns it into a linear equation.</p><p>Distinguish <strong>direct</strong> proportion, where one quantity rises as the other does (<em>y = kx</em>), from <strong>inverse</strong> proportion, where one falls as the other rises (<em>y = k/x</em>). More workers finishing a job faster is inverse, not direct.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Cross-multiply: <em>a/b = c/d</em> becomes <em>ad = bc</em>.</li><li>Keep corresponding quantities in matching positions.</li><li>Direct: <em>y = kx</em>, so y/x stays constant.</li><li>Inverse: <em>y = k/x</em>, so the product xy stays constant.</li><li>Similar figures give proportional corresponding sides.</li><li>Check the direction: does the answer being larger make sense?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">If 4 workers build a wall in 9 days, how long do 6 take? This is <strong>inverse</strong>: 4 &times; 9 = 36 worker-days, so 36 &divide; 6 = <strong>6 days</strong>. Treating it as direct would give 13.5 &mdash; more workers taking longer.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Proportional reasoning appears in geometry through similar triangles and throughout data analysis in scaling and rate questions.</p></div>",
  "Percent Problems": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Percent Problems</strong><br><span>Percent in context: discounts, interest, markups and change over time.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math10-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Percent Problems\"><figcaption>Percent Problems: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Part</strong><span>part = percent × whole</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Percent</strong><span>percent = part/whole × 100%</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Change</strong><span>new = original(1 ± rate)</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Percent Problems</summary><p>Problems involving parts per hundred, increases, decreases, or repeated change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dataset</summary><p>A collection of values gathered for analysis.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A characteristic or quantity that can change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>interpretation</summary><p>A conclusion drawn carefully from mathematical evidence.</p></details></section><p>Module 7 covered the arithmetic; here percentages appear inside situations. The three commonest are a discount then tax, simple versus compound interest, and percent change between two reported figures.</p><p>Compound interest is exponential: <em>A = P(1 + r)<sup>t</sup></em>. Simple interest is linear: <em>A = P(1 + rt)</em>. The SAT tests the difference deliberately.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Discount of d% then tax of t%: multiply by (1&minus;d) then (1+t).</li><li>Successive percentages multiply; they never add.</li><li>Compound: <em>A = P(1 + r)<sup>t</sup></em>. Simple: <em>A = P(1 + rt)</em>.</li><li>Percent change = (new &minus; old) &divide; old &times; 100.</li><li>To find the original before an increase, <strong>divide</strong> by (1 + rate).</li><li>Percentage points and percent are different: 20% to 25% is 5 points but a 25% increase.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An item priced $200 is discounted 15% then taxed 8%: 200 &times; 0.85 &times; 1.08 = <strong>$183.60</strong>. Combining into a single 7% reduction gives $186 &mdash; close enough to be tempting and wrong.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Percent-in-context problems are among the most frequent in Problem Solving and Data Analysis, and the multiply-don't-add rule settles most of them.</p></div>",
  "Statistics": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Statistics</strong><br><span>Mean, median, mode and spread &mdash; and knowing which one an outlier moves.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math10-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Statistics\"><figcaption>Statistics: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Mean</strong><span>sum of values ÷ number of values</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Median</strong><span>middle value after sorting</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Spread</strong><span>range = maximum − minimum</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Statistics</summary><p>Methods for describing, organizing, and interpreting data.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dataset</summary><p>A collection of values gathered for analysis.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A characteristic or quantity that can change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>interpretation</summary><p>A conclusion drawn carefully from mathematical evidence.</p></details></section><p>The <strong>mean</strong> is the average, the <strong>median</strong> the middle value when ordered, the <strong>mode</strong> the most frequent. The <strong>range</strong> is largest minus smallest, and <strong>standard deviation</strong> measures how spread out values are.</p><p>The SAT's favourite point here is that the mean is pulled by extreme values while the median is not. Questions about skewed data almost always turn on that.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Mean = sum &divide; count. Median = middle of the ordered list.</li><li>With an even count, the median is the average of the two middle values.</li><li>An <strong>outlier moves the mean, not the median</strong>.</li><li>Standard deviation measures spread; a tighter cluster means a smaller value.</li><li>Adding the same constant to every value shifts the mean but not the spread.</li><li>You are never asked to compute standard deviation, only to compare it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Incomes 20, 22, 24, 26, 400 (thousands): the median is <strong>24</strong> and the mean is 98.4. One extreme value has made the mean describe nobody in the list. That is why median is reported for incomes.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Statistics questions in Problem Solving and Data Analysis are usually conceptual rather than computational, so understanding beats arithmetic here.</p></div>",
  "Probability": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Probability</strong><br><span>Favourable outcomes over total outcomes &mdash; and read the table carefully.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math10-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Probability\"><figcaption>Probability: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Basic</strong><span>P(event)=favorable outcomes/total outcomes</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Complement</strong><span>P(not A)=1−P(A)</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Range</strong><span>0 ≤ P(A) ≤ 1</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Probability</summary><p>A numerical measure of how likely an event is.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dataset</summary><p>A collection of values gathered for analysis.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A characteristic or quantity that can change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>interpretation</summary><p>A conclusion drawn carefully from mathematical evidence.</p></details></section><p>Probability is the number of favourable outcomes divided by the total number of equally likely outcomes, always between 0 and 1. The probability that an event does <em>not</em> happen is 1 minus the probability that it does.</p><p>Most SAT probability questions are two-way table questions. The difficulty is not the arithmetic but identifying the correct denominator: the whole table, one row, or one column.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>P(event) = favourable &divide; total, always between 0 and 1.</li><li>P(not A) = 1 &minus; P(A).</li><li>For independent events, multiply: P(A and B) = P(A) &times; P(B).</li><li>In two-way tables, the wording fixes the denominator.</li><li>&ldquo;Of those who&hellip;&rdquo; restricts you to that row or column.</li><li>Express the answer as the question asks: fraction, decimal or percent.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A table shows 40 students, 25 of them female, 10 of those female students studying science. P(science <em>given</em> female) = 10/25 = <strong>0.4</strong>, not 10/40. The words &ldquo;of the female students&rdquo; changed the denominator.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Two-way table probability appears in nearly every Math section, and the denominator error is the single most common mistake made on it.</p></div>",
  "Data Interpretation": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Data Interpretation</strong><br><span>Read what the graphic shows, not what you expect it to show.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math10-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Data Interpretation\"><figcaption>Data Interpretation: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Read</strong><span>Identify variables, units, and sample.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Calculate</strong><span>Find the requested difference, rate, or trend.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Conclude</strong><span>Use only what the data supports.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Data Interpretation</summary><p>The process of extracting accurate meaning from presented data.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dataset</summary><p>A collection of values gathered for analysis.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A characteristic or quantity that can change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>interpretation</summary><p>A conclusion drawn carefully from mathematical evidence.</p></details></section><p>Data questions give you a table, graph or chart and ask what it supports. No advanced mathematics is needed; the marks are lost to careless reading of labels, units and scales.</p><p>A broken or non-zero axis exaggerates differences visually. A logarithmic scale compresses them. Always check where the axis starts before judging how big a change looks.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Read title, axis labels, units and legend before the choices.</li><li>Check where the vertical axis starts &mdash; a truncated axis exaggerates change.</li><li>Counts, percentages and rates are different things.</li><li>Only conclude what the data shows; correlation is not causation.</li><li>A trend over the shown period says nothing about outside it.</li><li>For &ldquo;consistent with the data&rdquo;, verify the numbers rather than the wording.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A bar chart with a vertical axis starting at 95 makes 96 look twice 95. The visual difference is a scale artefact; the actual difference is about 1%. The SAT offers a choice describing it as a &ldquo;dramatic increase&rdquo;.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Data interpretation appears in both Math and Reading &amp; Writing, and rewards nothing but disciplined reading of the labels.</p></div>",
  "Tables and Charts": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Tables and Charts</strong><br><span>Two-way tables reward finding the right row, column and total.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math10-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Tables and Charts\"><figcaption>Tables and Charts: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Table</strong><span>Follow the correct row and column.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Bar chart</strong><span>Compare category heights using the scale.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Line graph</strong><span>Track change across the horizontal axis.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Tables and Charts</summary><p>Structured visual displays used to organize and compare values.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dataset</summary><p>A collection of values gathered for analysis.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A characteristic or quantity that can change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>interpretation</summary><p>A conclusion drawn carefully from mathematical evidence.</p></details></section><p>A two-way table cross-classifies data by two variables. Every question about one is answered by identifying three numbers: the count you want, the relevant total, and the relationship between them.</p><p>Row totals, column totals and the grand total are all present. The wording of the question tells you which is the denominator, and getting that wrong is the standard error.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Identify the row, the column and the total the question needs.</li><li>&ldquo;Of all participants&rdquo; &rarr; grand total. &ldquo;Of the men&rdquo; &rarr; that row's total.</li><li>Check that row and column totals add to the grand total before relying on them.</li><li>Bar charts compare categories; line graphs show change over time; pie charts show shares of a whole.</li><li>A pie chart's slices must sum to 100%.</li><li>Convert to the form the question asks for: fraction, decimal or percentage.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A table shows 120 respondents, 70 rural, and 42 of the rural group owning a phone. &ldquo;What percentage of <em>rural</em> respondents own a phone?&rdquo; is 42/70 = <strong>60%</strong>. Using 120 gives 35%, which answers a different question.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Table questions are among the most numerous in Problem Solving and Data Analysis, and every one turns on choosing the correct denominator.</p></div>",
  "Scatterplots": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Scatterplots</strong><br><span>Points show a relationship; the line of best fit summarises it.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math10-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Scatterplots\"><figcaption>Scatterplots: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Direction</strong><span>Positive, negative, or no association.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Strength</strong><span>How closely points follow a pattern.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Model</strong><span>Line of best fit estimates values—not certainty.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Scatterplots</summary><p>Graphs showing the relationship between two quantitative variables.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dataset</summary><p>A collection of values gathered for analysis.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A characteristic or quantity that can change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>interpretation</summary><p>A conclusion drawn carefully from mathematical evidence.</p></details></section><p>A scatterplot plots one variable against another to reveal association. The <strong>line of best fit</strong> models the overall trend, and its slope is the predicted change in y for each unit increase in x.</p><p>Association is not causation. A strong pattern shows the two variables move together; it says nothing about which, if either, causes the other.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Positive association rises left to right; negative falls; no association shows no pattern.</li><li>The line of best fit models the trend, not any individual point.</li><li>Its slope = predicted change in y per one-unit increase in x.</li><li>Its y-intercept = predicted y when x is zero, which may be meaningless in context.</li><li>A <strong>residual</strong> is actual minus predicted; a point above the line has a positive residual.</li><li>Correlation never establishes causation.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A scatterplot of fertiliser against yield has a best-fit line of slope 0.8. Each additional unit of fertiliser predicts <strong>0.8 more tonnes</strong>. A field yielding 3 tonnes above the line has a positive residual of 3.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Scatterplots and lines of best fit recur throughout Problem Solving and Data Analysis, and the slope-as-rate interpretation is tested every time.</p></div>",
  "Real-Life Applications": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Real-Life Applications</strong><br><span>Context questions test whether you can read a situation into a model.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math10-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Real-Life Applications\"><figcaption>Real-Life Applications: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Define</strong><span>Name quantities and units.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Model</strong><span>Choose a ratio, percent, equation, or graph.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Interpret</strong><span>Return the numerical result to the situation.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Real-Life Applications</summary><p>Mathematical models used to solve practical situations.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dataset</summary><p>A collection of values gathered for analysis.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>variable</summary><p>A characteristic or quantity that can change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>interpretation</summary><p>A conclusion drawn carefully from mathematical evidence.</p></details></section><p>These questions wrap the mathematics in a scenario: budgets, fuel, wages, population, dosage. The work is identifying which model applies and what each number in it means.</p><p>Interpretation questions are common: given a model, what does a particular coefficient represent? The answer is always a rate, a starting value, or a total, and the units tell you which.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Fixed amount plus a rate &rarr; linear: <em>y = mx + b</em>.</li><li>Constant percentage change &rarr; exponential: <em>y = a&middot;b<sup>x</sup></em>.</li><li>The constant term is usually a starting or fixed value.</li><li>The coefficient on the variable is usually a rate &mdash; check its units.</li><li>Confirm your answer is plausible in the real situation.</li><li>Answer the question asked, which is often not the variable you solved for.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A generator model is <em>C = 45t + 300</em>, cost in dollars for t hours. The 300 is the fixed hire charge and the 45 is <strong>dollars per hour</strong> of running. Reading the units off the equation answers the interpretation question directly.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Modelling and interpretation questions are heavily represented on the Digital SAT, and they reward reading comprehension applied to algebra rather than harder algebra.</p></div>",
  "Lines and Angles": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Lines and Angles</strong><br><span>A handful of angle relationships settles most geometry setups.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math11-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Lines and Angles\"><figcaption>Lines and Angles: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Straight line</strong><span>Angles sum to 180°.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Around a point</strong><span>Angles sum to 360°.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Parallel lines</strong><span>Corresponding angles are equal.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Lines and Angles</summary><p>Geometric relationships involving straight paths, intersections, and rotations.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dimension</summary><p>A measurable length, width, height, or radius.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>theorem</summary><p>A mathematical statement proved to be true.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>diagram</summary><p>A labeled drawing representing geometric information.</p></details></section><p>Angles on a straight line sum to 180&deg;; angles round a point sum to 360&deg;. Vertical angles &mdash; opposite each other where two lines cross &mdash; are equal.</p><p>When a transversal cuts parallel lines, corresponding and alternate angles are equal, and co-interior angles sum to 180&deg;. Most SAT geometry diagrams are built from exactly these facts.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Angles on a straight line sum to <strong>180&deg;</strong>.</li><li>Angles around a point sum to <strong>360&deg;</strong>.</li><li>Vertical (opposite) angles are equal.</li><li>With parallel lines: corresponding and alternate angles are equal.</li><li>Co-interior (same-side) angles sum to 180&deg;.</li><li>Complementary angles sum to 90&deg;; supplementary to 180&deg;.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A transversal crosses two parallel lines and one angle is 70&deg;. Its vertical angle is 70&deg;, its corresponding angle is 70&deg;, and each co-interior angle is <strong>110&deg;</strong> &mdash; the whole diagram from one measurement.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Angle relationships are the entry point to nearly every geometry question, including triangle and circle problems that look harder than they are.</p></div>",
  "Triangles": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Triangles</strong><br><span>Angle sum, Pythagoras and similarity cover almost every triangle question.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math11-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Triangles\"><figcaption>Triangles: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Angle sum</strong><span>A+B+C=180°</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Area</strong><span>A=½bh</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Pythagorean</strong><span>a²+b²=c² for right triangles</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Triangles</summary><p>Three-sided polygons governed by angle, side, and area relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dimension</summary><p>A measurable length, width, height, or radius.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>theorem</summary><p>A mathematical statement proved to be true.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>diagram</summary><p>A labeled drawing representing geometric information.</p></details></section><p>A triangle's angles sum to 180&deg;. An exterior angle equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles. In a right triangle, <em>a&sup2; + b&sup2; = c&sup2;</em>, with c the hypotenuse.</p><p><strong>Similar</strong> triangles have equal angles and proportional sides &mdash; the basis of most multi-step geometry questions. The Pythagorean theorem and special right triangles are on the Bluebook reference sheet.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Interior angles sum to 180&deg;.</li><li>An exterior angle equals the sum of the two remote interior angles.</li><li>Pythagoras: <em>a&sup2; + b&sup2; = c&sup2;</em>, c opposite the right angle.</li><li>Common triples: 3-4-5, 5-12-13, 8-15-17 and their multiples.</li><li>Special triangles: 30-60-90 sides are <em>x</em>, <em>x&radic;3</em>, <em>2x</em>; 45-45-90 are <em>x</em>, <em>x</em>, <em>x&radic;2</em>.</li><li>Similar triangles have proportional corresponding sides.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A right triangle has legs 6 and 8. Rather than computing, recognise 6-8-10 as a doubled 3-4-5 triple: the hypotenuse is <strong>10</strong>. Recognising triples saves time on a section where time is the constraint.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Triangles appear in coordinate geometry, trigonometry and area questions, so the same few facts get reused throughout the geometry domain.</p></div>",
  "Circles": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Circles</strong><br><span>Radius drives everything; the reference sheet supplies the formulas.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math11-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Circles\"><figcaption>Circles: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Circumference</strong><span>C=2πr</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Area</strong><span>A=πr²</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Arc</strong><span>arc length = central angle/360 × 2πr</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Circles</summary><p>Sets of points equally distant from a center.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dimension</summary><p>A measurable length, width, height, or radius.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>theorem</summary><p>A mathematical statement proved to be true.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>diagram</summary><p>A labeled drawing representing geometric information.</p></details></section><p>Area is <em>&pi;r&sup2;</em> and circumference is <em>2&pi;r</em>, both on the Bluebook reference sheet. Arc length and sector area are fractions of those, set by the central angle over 360&deg;.</p><p>In coordinate geometry, a circle centred at (h, k) with radius r has equation <em>(x&minus;h)&sup2; + (y&minus;k)&sup2; = r&sup2;</em>. This equation is <strong>not</strong> on the reference sheet.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Area = <em>&pi;r&sup2;</em>; circumference = <em>2&pi;r</em>; diameter = 2r.</li><li>Arc length = (angle/360) &times; circumference.</li><li>Sector area = (angle/360) &times; area.</li><li>Circle equation: <em>(x&minus;h)&sup2; + (y&minus;k)&sup2; = r&sup2;</em> &mdash; memorise it.</li><li>A radius drawn to the point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent.</li><li>An inscribed angle is half the central angle on the same arc.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A circle of radius 6 with a 60&deg; sector: the sector is 60/360 = 1/6 of the circle, so its area is <em>(1/6)&pi;(36)</em> = <strong>6&pi;</strong>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Circle questions appear in both plane and coordinate geometry, and the circle equation is one of the few geometry formulas you must actually memorise.</p></div>",
  "Polygons": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Polygons</strong><br><span>Angle sums follow one formula; area usually means splitting the shape up.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math11-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Polygons\"><figcaption>Polygons: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Interior sum</strong><span>(n−2)180°</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Regular interior</strong><span>((n−2)180°)/n</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Exterior sum</strong><span>360°</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Polygons</summary><p>Closed plane figures made from straight line segments.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dimension</summary><p>A measurable length, width, height, or radius.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>theorem</summary><p>A mathematical statement proved to be true.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>diagram</summary><p>A labeled drawing representing geometric information.</p></details></section><p>The interior angles of an n-sided polygon sum to <em>(n &minus; 2) &times; 180&deg;</em>. In a <strong>regular</strong> polygon all angles are equal, so each is that sum divided by n. Exterior angles always sum to 360&deg;, whatever n is.</p><p>For area, the reference sheet gives rectangles, triangles and circles. Anything else you split into those shapes.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Interior angle sum = <em>(n &minus; 2) &times; 180&deg;</em>.</li><li>Each interior angle of a regular n-gon = <em>(n &minus; 2) &times; 180&deg; &divide; n</em>.</li><li>Exterior angles of any polygon sum to <strong>360&deg;</strong>.</li><li>Split irregular shapes into rectangles and triangles to find area.</li><li>A parallelogram's area is base &times; height, using perpendicular height.</li><li>A trapezoid's area is the average of the parallel sides times the height.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A regular hexagon: n = 6, so the interior sum is (6&minus;2) &times; 180 = 720&deg;, and each angle is 720 &divide; 6 = <strong>120&deg;</strong>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Polygon questions are formula-driven and quick once the angle-sum relation is memorised, which makes them dependable points in the geometry domain.</p></div>",
  "Coordinate Geometry": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Coordinate Geometry</strong><br><span>Algebra applied to the plane: distance, midpoint, slope and equations.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math11-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Coordinate Geometry\"><figcaption>Coordinate Geometry: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Distance</strong><span>√((x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²)</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Midpoint</strong><span>((x₁+x₂)/2,(y₁+y₂)/2)</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Slope</strong><span>(y₂−y₁)/(x₂−x₁)</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Coordinate Geometry</summary><p>Study of geometric figures using points, equations, and a coordinate plane.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dimension</summary><p>A measurable length, width, height, or radius.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>theorem</summary><p>A mathematical statement proved to be true.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>diagram</summary><p>A labeled drawing representing geometric information.</p></details></section><p>The distance between two points comes from Pythagoras: <em>&radic;((x&#8322;&minus;x&#8321;)&sup2; + (y&#8322;&minus;y&#8321;)&sup2;)</em>. The midpoint is the average of the coordinates.</p><p>Slope, parallel and perpendicular relationships, and the circle equation all appear here. None of these formulas is on the reference sheet, so they have to be learned.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Distance = <em>&radic;((x&#8322;&minus;x&#8321;)&sup2; + (y&#8322;&minus;y&#8321;)&sup2;)</em>.</li><li>Midpoint = <em>((x&#8321;+x&#8322;)/2, (y&#8321;+y&#8322;)/2)</em>.</li><li>Parallel lines share a slope; perpendicular slopes multiply to &minus;1.</li><li>Circle: <em>(x&minus;h)&sup2; + (y&minus;k)&sup2; = r&sup2;</em>.</li><li>The x-intercept has y = 0; the y-intercept has x = 0.</li><li>Desmos plots any of these instantly for checking.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Distance from (1, 2) to (4, 6): differences 3 and 4, so <em>&radic;(9 + 16)</em> = <strong>5</strong>. Recognising the 3-4-5 triple gets you there without touching the calculator.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Coordinate geometry links the Algebra and Geometry domains, and its formulas are among the few the app does not supply.</p></div>",
  "Right Triangle Trigonometry": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Right Triangle Trigonometry</strong><br><span>SOH-CAH-TOA, and the ratio you need is decided by which sides you have.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math11-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Right Triangle Trigonometry\"><figcaption>Right Triangle Trigonometry: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Sine</strong><span>sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Cosine</strong><span>cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Tangent</strong><span>tan θ = opposite/adjacent</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Right Triangle Trigonometry</summary><p>Use of side ratios to connect angles and lengths in right triangles.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dimension</summary><p>A measurable length, width, height, or radius.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>theorem</summary><p>A mathematical statement proved to be true.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>diagram</summary><p>A labeled drawing representing geometric information.</p></details></section><p>In a right triangle, <em>sin&theta; = opposite/hypotenuse</em>, <em>cos&theta; = adjacent/hypotenuse</em>, <em>tan&theta; = opposite/adjacent</em>. Label the sides relative to the angle you are using, not to the page.</p><p>The SAT also tests the complementary relationship: <em>sin&theta; = cos(90&deg; &minus; &theta;)</em>. That identity appears as a question in its own right.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>SOH-CAH-TOA</strong> &mdash; sine, cosine and tangent as side ratios.</li><li>Label opposite and adjacent relative to the angle in question.</li><li><em>sin&theta; = cos(90&deg; &minus; &theta;)</em> for complementary angles.</li><li>Use the ratio matching the two sides you have or want.</li><li>Special triangles give exact values: sin30&deg; = 1/2, sin45&deg; = &radic;2/2, sin60&deg; = &radic;3/2.</li><li>Trigonometric ratios apply to right triangles only.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A right triangle has an opposite side of 3 and a hypotenuse of 5. <em>sin&theta; = 3/5</em>. The adjacent side is 4 (a 3-4-5 triple), so <em>cos&theta; = 4/5</em> and <em>tan&theta; = <strong>3/4</strong></em>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Trigonometry is a small but reliable part of the Geometry and Trigonometry domain, and the complementary-angle identity is tested more often than any computation.</p></div>",
  "Area and Volume": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Area and Volume</strong><br><span>The formulas are provided; knowing which one applies is the skill.</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual sat-visual sat-math11-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital SAT guide for Area and Volume\"><figcaption>Area and Volume: read, calculate and verify</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Rectangle</strong><span>A=lw</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Cylinder</strong><span>V=πr²h</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Composite figure</strong><span>Divide into known shapes, then combine.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Area and Volume</summary><p>Measures of two-dimensional surface and three-dimensional space.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>dimension</summary><p>A measurable length, width, height, or radius.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>theorem</summary><p>A mathematical statement proved to be true.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>diagram</summary><p>A labeled drawing representing geometric information.</p></details></section><p>The Bluebook reference sheet supplies areas of rectangles, triangles and circles, and volumes of rectangular solids, cylinders, spheres, cones and pyramids. You do not need to memorise them.</p><p>What you do need is to identify the solid, extract the right measurements, and watch the scaling rule: multiplying every length by k multiplies area by k&sup2; and volume by k&sup3;.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Reference sheet supplies area and volume formulas &mdash; use it.</li><li>Read whether a question gives radius or <strong>diameter</strong>; halve if needed.</li><li>Length scale factor k &rarr; area &times; k&sup2;, volume &times; k&sup3;.</li><li>Surface area is the sum of the faces; volume is the space inside.</li><li>Composite solids: split them, compute each part, then add.</li><li>Keep units consistent before calculating, and state units in the answer.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Double every dimension of a cube and the volume becomes <strong>8 times</strong> larger, not twice. A 2&times;2&times;2 cube has volume 8; a 4&times;4&times;4 cube has volume 64.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Area and volume questions are usually about setup rather than formula recall, and the k&sup2;/k&sup3; scaling rule is tested as a concept question every time it appears.</p></div>",
  "Time Management": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Time Management</strong><br><span>The clock is the same for everyone; pacing is what differs.</span></div><p>Reading &amp; Writing gives 27 questions in 32 minutes, roughly <strong>70 seconds</strong> each. Math gives 22 questions in 35 minutes, roughly <strong>95 seconds</strong> each. Questions within a module are broadly ordered by difficulty in Math and grouped by type in Reading &amp; Writing.</p><p>The single most valuable habit is abandoning a question you are stuck on. Flag it, move on, and return. Two easy questions later in the module are worth more than one hard one you are determined to finish.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Reading &amp; Writing: about 70 seconds per question.</li><li>Math: about 95 seconds per question.</li><li>Flag and move on after roughly double your target time.</li><li>Every question is worth the same mark, whatever its difficulty.</li><li>Reach the end of the module &mdash; unseen questions score nothing.</li><li>Leave two minutes to answer everything still blank.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A student spends four minutes on question 9 of Math and never reaches questions 20&ndash;22. He got question 9 right. He lost three marks to gain one, and two of those three were easier than the one he solved.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Pacing costs nothing to learn and is worth more marks than most content revision, because unanswered questions are guaranteed zeros while guesses are not.</p></div>",
  "Adaptive Testing Strategy": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Adaptive Testing Strategy</strong><br><span>Module 1 sets your ceiling, so spend your best effort there.</span></div><p>Each section's module 1 is routed: perform well and module 2 comes from the harder pool, which is scaled to reach the top of the 200&ndash;800 range. Perform poorly and module 2 is easier, with a lower ceiling.</p><p>The practical consequence is counterintuitive. Conventional advice says spread your effort evenly. On the Digital SAT, module 1 accuracy matters more than module 2 accuracy, so never rush module 1 to bank time.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Module 1 routes you to the easier or harder module 2.</li><li>Only the harder module 2 reaches the upper score range.</li><li>Never rush module 1 to save time &mdash; there is no time carried over.</li><li>The sections adapt independently of each other.</li><li>If module 2 feels hard, that is usually a good sign.</li><li>Accuracy in module 1 beats speed in module 1.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Two students each answer 30 of 54 Reading &amp; Writing questions correctly. The one who concentrated her accuracy in module 1 was routed to the harder module 2 and scored higher. Identical totals, different scores.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>This is the single strategic difference between the Digital SAT and every paper test before it, and most preparation material written before 2023 gets it wrong.</p></div>",
  "Guessing Techniques": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Guessing Techniques</strong><br><span>No penalty for a wrong answer means never leaving a blank.</span></div><p>The SAT subtracts nothing for an incorrect response. A blank scores zero with certainty; a guess among four options scores 25% of the time, and better than that after eliminating even one choice.</p><p>Eliminate before guessing. Removing one option raises your odds to one in three, and two to one in two. On student-produced response questions, enter a plausible value rather than nothing.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>There is <strong>no penalty</strong> for a wrong answer &mdash; never leave a blank.</li><li>Blind guessing among four is 25%; after one elimination, 33%; after two, 50%.</li><li>Use the Bluebook option eliminator to cross out as you reason.</li><li>Extreme or absolute wording is usually eliminable in Reading &amp; Writing.</li><li>In Math, discard answers of implausible magnitude or the wrong sign.</li><li>For student-produced responses, type a reasonable value rather than leaving it empty.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">With two minutes left and three questions unanswered, filling all three blindly is worth about 0.75 marks on average. Leaving them is worth exactly zero. It costs ten seconds.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>This is the cheapest advice on the test and the most frequently ignored. Candidates trained on penalty-scored exams still leave blanks out of habit.</p></div>",
  "Eliminating Wrong Answers": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Eliminating Wrong Answers</strong><br><span>Finding three wrong answers is often easier than finding one right one.</span></div><p>Every multiple-choice question has three distractors, and they are constructed rather than random. Learning what they look like lets you remove them quickly and confidently.</p><p>In Reading &amp; Writing, distractors are typically too extreme, outside the passage, or true-but-irrelevant. In Math, they are the result of a predictable error &mdash; a sign flip, a missed step, or answering the wrong question.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Reading &amp; Writing distractors: too extreme, not in the passage, true but off-point.</li><li>Math distractors: sign errors, half-finished work, the other variable's value.</li><li>Use the Bluebook option eliminator so ruled-out choices stay ruled out.</li><li>If two choices say the same thing differently, neither is usually right.</li><li>In Math, check magnitude and sign before doing any arithmetic.</li><li>Elimination plus a guess beats a blank every time.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A question asks for y after solving a system. The value of <strong>x</strong> is almost always among the choices, because the test knows some candidates will stop a step early. Checking what was asked eliminates it instantly.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Elimination is a general-purpose tool that works when your method fails, which is exactly when you need it most.</p></div>",
  "Common Mistakes": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Common Mistakes</strong><br><span>Most lost marks come from a short list of avoidable habits.</span></div><p>Scores are rarely lost to material a candidate never learned. They are lost to answering the wrong question, mis-copying a number, flipping a sign, leaving blanks, and spending four minutes on one item.</p><p>The remedy is a checking habit rather than more content revision: before submitting an answer, confirm it answers what was asked and is plausible.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Answering the wrong question &mdash; x when y was asked, or the change when the total was.</li><li>Sign errors, especially when distributing a minus across brackets.</li><li>Misreading units, or radius given as diameter.</li><li>Leaving blanks despite there being no penalty.</li><li>Over-investing in one hard question and not reaching later easy ones.</li><li>Ignoring the qualifiers <em>not</em>, <em>except</em> and <em>least</em> in a question stem.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A student solves a percent question correctly and reports the new price when the question asked for the <strong>increase</strong>. Full method, correct arithmetic, no mark. The error was in the last two seconds.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Reviewing an error log by <em>reason</em> rather than by topic usually reveals that two or three habits account for most lost marks &mdash; and habits are faster to fix than knowledge gaps.</p></div>",
  "Stress Management": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Stress Management</strong><br><span>A calm candidate reads the question correctly; that is most of the benefit.</span></div><p>Test anxiety costs marks through mechanical errors: misreading stems, rushing, and abandoning a method halfway. Preparation reduces it more than any technique, because familiarity removes surprise.</p><p>In the room, a slow breath and a deliberate re-read of the question is usually enough to recover. If a module has gone badly, put it down: the next module is scored on its own.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Full timed practice reduces anxiety more than any relaxation technique.</li><li>Slow breathing steadies attention: in for four, out for six.</li><li>Re-read the stem when you feel rushed &mdash; misreading is the main cost of panic.</li><li>Do not carry a bad module into the next one; they are scored separately.</li><li>Sleep normally for several nights, not just the night before.</li><li>Avoid last-minute cramming on the morning of the test.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A candidate blanks on a Math question, breathes slowly, re-reads, and notices the question gave a <em>diameter</em> where she had used it as a radius. Thirty seconds of calm recovered a mark she had already lost.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Anxiety is not a character problem; it is a preparation and technique problem, and both respond to practice under realistic conditions.</p></div>",
  "Test-Day Checklist": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Test-Day Checklist</strong><br><span>Everything that can be settled the night before, settle the night before.</span></div><p>The morning of the test is the worst possible time to discover a flat battery, a missing admission ticket or a name mismatch on your ID. Assemble everything the evening before and leave it by the door.</p><p>Complete Bluebook exam setup days ahead, not on the morning. Charge your device fully and bring the charger anyway.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Passport matching your registration, plus the admission ticket.</li><li>Device fully charged, Bluebook installed, <strong>exam setup already completed</strong>.</li><li>Charger, and an approved calculator if you prefer your own to Desmos.</li><li>Phone and smart watch off and stored &mdash; through the break too.</li><li>Arrive at least 30 minutes early; latecomers are not admitted.</li><li>Eat beforehand; the test runs over two hours with one short break.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">The night before: passport, ticket, charged laptop, charger and water by the door. Bluebook setup done three days earlier. Alarm set for a departure 30 minutes earlier than strictly necessary. Nothing left to decide in the morning.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>None of this improves your English or your maths. All of it protects the score those months of work have already earned.</p></div>",
  "SAT Study Planner": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 SAT Study Planner</strong><br><span>A written plan that survives a bad week is better than a perfect one that does not.</span></div><p>Build the plan backwards from your test date. Reserve the final week for review and one taper, the fortnight before that for full mocks, and everything earlier for targeted practice on your weaker section.</p><p>Write it down and keep it visible. A plan held in your head quietly becomes &ldquo;whatever I feel like studying tonight&rdquo;, which is always the section you are already best at.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Work backwards from the test date, reserving the last week for review.</li><li>Give the weaker section roughly twice the time.</li><li>Schedule specific topics, not &ldquo;do some Math&rdquo;.</li><li>Build in two rest days a week &mdash; a plan you abandon is worthless.</li><li>Re-diagnose every fortnight and adjust the allocation.</li><li>Keep the plan and the error log in the same place.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An eight-week plan: weeks 1&ndash;2 fundamentals in the weaker section, 3&ndash;5 topic drills across both, 6&ndash;7 timed modules, week 8 a final mock five days out then review only. Two rest days weekly throughout.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The plan is what converts a distant 1600-point scale into tonight's specific task, and the fortnightly re-diagnosis is what stops it drifting toward comfortable work.</p></div>",
  "Formula Sheet": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Formula Sheet</strong><br><span>Memorise the short list the app does not give you.</span></div><p>Bluebook provides geometry formulas on screen. It does not provide algebra, statistics or rate formulas. That asymmetry decides what is worth memorising.</p><p>The list below is complete for the Digital SAT. It is short, and knowing it removes the temptation to spend revision time on formulas the app will hand you anyway.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Slope: <em>m = (y&#8322;&minus;y&#8321;)/(x&#8322;&minus;x&#8321;)</em>.</li><li>Quadratic formula: <em>x = (&minus;b &plusmn; &radic;(b&sup2;&minus;4ac)) / 2a</em>.</li><li>Distance: <em>&radic;((x&#8322;&minus;x&#8321;)&sup2; + (y&#8322;&minus;y&#8321;)&sup2;)</em>; midpoint: averages of the coordinates.</li><li>Circle: <em>(x&minus;h)&sup2; + (y&minus;k)&sup2; = r&sup2;</em>.</li><li>Percent change = (new &minus; old) &divide; old &times; 100; mean = sum &divide; count.</li><li>Exponential model <em>y = a&middot;b<sup>x</sup></em>; compound interest <em>A = P(1+r)<sup>t</sup></em>.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Print this list, keep it with your error log, and read it before every practice session. Geometry areas and volumes are deliberately absent &mdash; the reference sheet supplies them during the test.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Memorising the provided formulas wastes revision time; memorising the unprovided ones prevents the specific stalls that cost marks under time pressure.</p></div>",
  "Grammar Cheat Sheet": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Grammar Cheat Sheet</strong><br><span>Every convention the SAT tests, on one page.</span></div><p>Standard English Conventions is drawn from a closed list. This page is that list, and it is worth reading before every practice session until the checks become automatic.</p><p>Work through an underlined portion by asking the questions in order: is it a clause boundary, an agreement, a pronoun, a modifier, a parallel list, or a word choice?</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Clause boundaries</strong>: two independent clauses need a period, semicolon, or comma + FANBOYS.</li><li><strong>Agreement</strong>: cross out phrases between subject and verb, then match.</li><li><strong>Pronouns</strong>: number match, correct case, unambiguous reference.</li><li><strong>Modifiers</strong>: an opening phrase describes the noun after the comma.</li><li><strong>Parallelism</strong>: list items and paired constructions share one form.</li><li><strong>Word choice</strong>: affect/effect, its/it's, fewer/less, then/than, plus idiomatic prepositions.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">The order matters. Checking clause boundaries first settles every punctuation question immediately; only if no boundary issue exists do you move on to agreement, pronouns and the rest.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Conventions is roughly a quarter of Reading &amp; Writing and is the most rule-bound part of the entire test, which makes this the highest-return page in the course to revise.</p></div>",
  "Vocabulary List (1,000+ Words)": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Vocabulary List (1,000+ Words)</strong><br><span>Learn academic words in sentences, and prioritise the ones that keep appearing.</span></div><p>The Digital SAT tests high-utility academic vocabulary in context, not rare words in isolation. A long list is only useful if it is worked through in phrases and revisited.</p><p>Prioritise words carrying a clear positive or negative charge, and words with a common meaning plus a distinct academic one &mdash; those are the two families the test draws on most.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Learn every word inside an example sentence, never as a bare definition.</li><li>Prioritise words with a common and an academic meaning: <em>novel, qualify, sustain, arbitrary</em>.</li><li>Note each word's charge: <em>undermine</em> negative, <em>bolster</em> positive.</li><li>Twenty words a week, revisited, beats two hundred skimmed once.</li><li>Group by theme &mdash; research, argument, change, causation.</li><li>Reading serious journalism daily is the most efficient supplement.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>Arbitrary</em> means based on choice rather than reason, and carries a negative charge. In <em>&ldquo;the boundary was arbitrary&rdquo;</em> the author is criticising it. Knowing the charge answers tone and purpose questions as well as vocabulary ones.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Because the test moved away from obscure words, vocabulary is now one of the most improvable areas &mdash; the words that appear are the ones academic writing uses constantly.</p></div>",
  "Practice Worksheets": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Practice Worksheets</strong><br><span>Practice counts only when it is timed, marked and reviewed.</span></div><p>Untimed practice with the answers nearby measures what you can do with help, which is not what the test reports. Set a timer, work without checking, mark honestly, then review.</p><p>The review is where the score comes from. For every wrong answer, write the <em>reason</em>: wrong question answered, sign error, misread unit, too-narrow choice, ran out of time.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Time every set from the first attempt; the clock is part of the skill.</li><li>Do not check answers mid-set &mdash; it inflates the result and teaches nothing.</li><li>Mark honestly, including student-produced responses that were nearly right.</li><li>Record the <strong>reason</strong> for each error, not just the correct letter.</li><li>Redo missed questions a week later, not immediately.</li><li>Two carefully reviewed sets beat five rushed ones.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A student scores 14/20, and reviewing shows four errors were sign slips in distributing a minus. That is one habit, not four topics &mdash; and it takes an evening to fix rather than a fortnight.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Reviewed practice is the only kind that raises a score. Unreviewed practice mostly rehearses the errors you already make.</p></div>",
  "Score Improvement Guide": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Score Improvement Guide</strong><br><span>Lift the weakest section first; the total is an average.</span></div><p>Because the total is the sum of two section scores, the fastest route upward is almost always the weaker section. Moving a 480 to 560 is far easier than moving a 690 to 720.</p><p>Within a section, target by question type rather than by topic. An error log grouped by reason usually shows two or three recurring habits accounting for most lost marks.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Work on the weaker section first &mdash; the total is a sum, not a maximum.</li><li>Target the question <strong>types</strong> costing you most, not topics you dislike.</li><li>Low scores usually improve through fundamentals; high scores through pacing and precision.</li><li>Grammar and conventions are the fastest points in Reading &amp; Writing.</li><li>Linear equations and percentages are the fastest points in Math.</li><li>Re-diagnose fortnightly; without measurement you are guessing.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A student at 520 Reading &amp; Writing and 610 Math spends six weeks on Math because he enjoys it, and gains 20 points. The same six weeks on conventions and vocabulary in context would have been worth considerably more.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Score improvement is an allocation problem before it is a study problem. Deciding where the effort goes matters more than how many hours it is.</p></div>",
  "College Admission Tips": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 College Admission Tips</strong><br><span>The score is one part of an application, and rarely the deciding part.</span></div><p>Colleges read transcripts, essays, recommendations and activities alongside the SAT. A strong score opens doors; it does not by itself win a place, and a middling score is often offset by a strong record elsewhere.</p><p>For international applicants, check each institution's requirements individually: some are test-optional, some require the SAT, and several use score bands for merit scholarships even when admission is test-optional.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Check each college's requirement <strong>individually</strong> and in writing.</li><li>Test-optional for admission does not always mean test-optional for scholarships.</li><li>Aim for the middle 50% range published by each college.</li><li>Scores are generally valid for five years.</li><li>Score Choice lets you decide which sitting to send, where a college permits it.</li><li>Deadlines for scores differ from application deadlines &mdash; check both.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A student is admitted test-optional but discovers the merit scholarship required a score by an earlier deadline. The place was never in doubt; the funding was, and it was decided by a date she had not checked.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Understanding where the SAT sits in the wider application prevents both over-investing in a score that will not move the decision and under-investing in one that funds the whole plan.</p></div>",
  "Certificate Requirements": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Certificate Requirements</strong><br><span>What you must complete to earn your TIH Certificate of Completion.</span></div><p>The certificate records that you worked through the whole course, not that you achieved a particular SAT score. It unlocks automatically in the player once every requirement is met.</p><p>Lessons unlock in order, so the requirements are met by working through the course as designed rather than by seeking anything out separately.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Complete all core lessons in <strong>Modules 1&ndash;12</strong>.</li><li>Attempt every module quiz and section mock test.</li><li>Complete the <strong>Midterm Examination</strong> in Module 13.</li><li>Complete at least <strong>two Full SAT Mock Tests</strong>.</li><li>Pass the <strong>Final Assessment</strong> with 70% or higher.</li><li>Retakes are unlimited, so a failed attempt costs nothing but time.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Once the Final Assessment is passed, the Certificate of Completion appears in the course player automatically. There is nothing to request and no waiting period.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The certificate documents completed preparation, which is useful evidence for a school, a sponsor or a scholarship application alongside the official College Board score report.</p></div>"
  };
})();
